{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2Vw2JDu9WuSr4QexXW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HR6DFJiMkBKFVNatzPTgFk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183153__xf5fb84/work/TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153 && export TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp && printf 'node_modules\\n.tmp\\nbuild\\nhubtest\\ncheck-output\\nshots\\n' > .gitignore && npm install --no-audit --no-fund 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Install Playwright dev dependency","timeout":300000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":902,"cache_read_input_tokens":141292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":902},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcbe5b02-218b-4276-a1cd-eeebf017e39a","uuid":"a1fb5252-fe63-484a-8930-564f9aa763f6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:22.746Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2VvmRitdCPYyw5VgmC","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HR6DFJiMkBKFVNatzPTgFk":{"command":"cd ~/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183153__xf5fb84/work/TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153 && export TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp && printf 'node_modules\\n.tmp\\nbuild\\nhubtest\\ncheck-output\\nshots\\n' > .gitignore && npm install --no-audit --no-fund 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Install Playwright dev dependency","timeout":300000}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HR6DFJiMkBKFVNatzPTgFk","type":"tool_result","content":"added 2 packages in 633ms","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcbe5b02-218b-4276-a1cd-eeebf017e39a","uuid":"8869342d-46dd-4f41-8e8a-c888efbb9a3d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:24.090Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"added 2 packages in 633ms","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":3,"newStart":22,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_0179m9mzcfnVZZDrAfrT3bfc\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t# Tide Crisis 3\\n2\\t\\n3\\t**The title.** As TASK.md asks, I brainstormed 50 riffs on *Time Crisis 3* without showing them, then picked one with an\\n4\\tOS-entropy RNG (`python3`, `secrets.randbelow(50)` → #9). In file names it is `TideCrisis3`, and the source is\\n5\\t`TimeCrisis3`.\\n6\\t\\n7\\t**The pitch.** Tide Crisis 3 is an original arcade light-gun shooter on rails, drawn in my-3D2dge's PS1-era pixel style\\n8\\tand played in first person. Two agents of C.R.E.S.T. (Covert Response & Emergency Strike Team) fight to take an island\\n9\\tcity's tidal barrier back from a disgraced admiral before the dawn king tide drowns the city. You take cover to dodge\\n10\\tand reload, then pop out to shoot. You switch between four weapons, read the \\\"crisis flash\\\" that marks every shot that\\n11\\tcan hit you, and beat the clock in every area. You can play alone, with two players on one machine (split screen), or\\n12\\twith 1–4 players online in an AI Hub session.\\n13\\t\\n14\\t**The core loop** (write this sentence at the top of the game code): *the camera rides the rail to the next area (WAIT)\\n15\\t→ ACTION! starts the clock → pop out and shoot → duck when a crisis flash says a shot will hit → reload in cover →\\n16\\tclear the area before TIME runs out → ride on → beat the boss → STAGE CLEAR.*\\n17\\t\\n18\\t---\\n19\\t\\n20\\t## Contents\\n21\\t0. Read this first: deliverables, rules, environment, glossary\\n22\\t1. The game: what we keep from the source, pillars, modes, length\\n23\\t2. Source information (verified at planning time)\\n24\\t3. Technology and architecture\\n25\\t4. Systems (each with its spec, numbers and tests)\\n26\\t5. Content: world, cast, tone, enemies, stages, bosses, script, art, animation, audio\\n27\\t6. Build order and milestones\\n28\\t7. Risks and how to de-risk them\\n29\\t8. Testing\\n30\\t9. Done checklist (every TASK.md requirement, and where this plan covers it)\\n31\\t\\n32\\t---\\n33\\t\\n34\\t## 0. Read this first\\n35\\t\\n36\\t### 0.1 Deliverables (exact names, all in this folder)\\n37\\t| What | Name |\\n38\\t|---|---|\\n39\\t| Dev folder (every dev file lives here) | `TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731/` |\\n40\\t| The game, one self-contained HTML file | `TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731.html` |\\n41\\t| Exit interview (exactly TASK.md's shape; check that it parses) | `exit-interview.json` |\\n42\\t\\n43\\tThe HTML inlines the patched engine, the game and the AI Hub session client. It plays from `file://` with no network.\\n44\\tSource Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in three places: the in-game About/Credits\\n45\\tscreen, `exit-interview.json`, and the final chat reply. Generate all three from one data file (`src/99-about.js`,\\n46\\t§4.14) so they agree.\\n47\\t\\n48\\t### 0.2 Golden rules\\n49\\t1. **Original world, original cast.** The source gives us a genre and a feel, nothing more (§1.1). Never use its names,\\n50\\t   levels, music, logos or UI art.\\n51\\t2. **The simulation knows nothing about rendering, and it is deterministic.** `Sim` holds the whole game state and\\n52\\t   steps it from player commands, with seeded RNG streams. The presentation layer (renderer, HUD, audio, FX) only reads\\n53\\t   it. Netcode, the headless tests, the bot and the attract mode all depend on this rule.\\n54\\t3. **Content is data, held in registries** (`def(kind, id, spec)`), and validators keep it honest (§4.10). Designers\\n55\\t   build the rest of the game later by adding entries, not by editing systems.\\n56\\t4. **No TODOs, placeholders or stubs.** If time runs short, cut whole scenes in the order in §6. Never cut a mechanic.\\n57\\t5. **Use the engine's standard features**: the pause menu, settings, control rebinding, full screen, the boot screen\\n58\\t   and auto-pause. Adjust them through `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`; don't rebuild them.\\n59\\t6. **Tests run behind one command** that prints only failures and a one-line count (§8). After a change, rerun only\\n60\\t   the suites that cover the code you changed.\\n61\\t7. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\\n62\\t\\n63\\t### 0.3 Environment notes\\n64\\t- Other agents share this machine. Read only what you need. Any server binds `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port\\n65\\t  (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it). Confirm the served page is your game before you trust a\\n66\\t  result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or a kill on whatever\\n67\\t  holds a port.\\n68\\t- **`/tmp` (a tmpfs) was 100% full at planning time.** `git clone` failed there with \\\"No space left on device\\\".\\n69\\t  Chromium and Playwright write their profiles to `TMPDIR`, so export `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp` (create it, and add it to\\n70\\t  `.gitignore`) before any browser tool runs.\\n71\\t- Playwright: the handoff pins `playwright` 1.63.0. A Chromium build (`chromium-1243`) is already in\\n72\\t  `~/.cache/ms-playwright`. If the versions don't match, run `npx playwright install chromium` with `TMPDIR` set.\\n73\\t- Put reference clones outside the shared `runs/` tree, as sparse checkouts in a scratch folder you own, e.g.\\n74\\t  `~/.cache/<run-id>/`. Take `dist/handoff` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge and `lib/hub` of\\n75\\t  github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub. Copy into the dev folder only the files you ship or run (§3.2). The handoff is 16 MB.\\n76\\t\\n77\\t### 0.4 Glossary\\n78\\t| Term | Meaning |\\n79\\t|---|---|\\n80\\t| Stage | One of the three levels (plus the Training range), each with its own world geometry, palette, music and boss |\\n81\\t| Scene (area) | One fight point in a stage: a time limit, player slots, enemy cover points, waves, cues. Shown to players as \\\"AREA 2\\\" |\\n82\\t| Slot | Where player *i* stands in a scene: a *stand* pose (exposed) and a *cover* pose (hidden), each a camera pose |\\n83\\t| Cover point | A spot an enemy hides behind and peeks out from |\\n84\\t| Wave | A group of spawns that starts at a time, at a distance travelled, or when an earlier wave is cleared |\\n85\\t| Threat | An enemy attack that will hit a player who is still exposed when it lands. Telegraphed by the crisis flash |\\n86\\t| Director | The per-scene runner: waves, cues, the clear condition |\\n87\\t| Rail | The camera paths between scenes (`E.Path`), and inside moving scenes |\\n88\\t| Carrier | A moving object a slot rides on (the boat, the funicular car, the tower lift) |\\n89\\t| View units (vu) | Resolution-free screen coordinates: `ax ∈ [−A, A]`, `ay ∈ [−1, 1]`, y up, where A is the viewport's aspect ratio |\\n90\\t| u | World units. 16 u = 1 m |\\n91\\t\\n92\\t---\\n93\\t\\n94\\t## 1. The game\\n95\\t\\n96\\t### 1.1 From the source into our game\\n97\\t| Distinctive in Time Crisis 3 (and the series) | What we build (original) |\\n98\\t|---|---|\\n99\\t| A foot pedal: release it to hide behind cover and reload, press it to stand and shoot | **Cover is the game.** Hold L2, right mouse button or Space to duck behind real geometry; let go to pop out. You are invulnerable in cover, and you can only reload there. Toggle mode is a setting. |\\n100\\t| The series' first weapon-select system: a 9-round handgun with unlimited ammo, a machine gun, a shotgun, a grenade launcher | The same four weapon roles with our own names and numbers (§4.4). Fire while hidden cycles weapons, so a player with only a trigger can switch; R1/L1, the mouse wheel and the 1–4 keys also switch. |\\n101\\t| Shooting yellow-clad soldiers refills special-weapon ammo | **Quartermasters in orange life vests** carry ammo crates. Kill one and you get special ammo at once. |\\n102\\t| The crisis flash: shots that will hit you flash brighter, so you know to duck | **Crisis flash**: every threat shows a shrinking red ring with a white core and a \\\"!\\\" glyph at the shooter's muzzle, a muzzle flare, a rising tick sound, and an arrow at the screen edge if the shooter is off screen. Only threats can hurt you; misses are spectacle. |\\n103\\t| Red-uniformed soldiers (series tradition) who shoot fast and accurately | **Red Tide elites**: crimson coats, short telegraphs, every shot a threat. |\\n104\\t| A time limit on every area | **TIME** on every scene, drawn as a rising-tide gauge. When it runs out (\\\"TIDE'S IN!\\\") you lose a life. |\\n105\\t| Lives, continues, arcade score | Lives, 10-second continues, score, a chain multiplier, ranks, high-score initials. |\\n106\\t| Two-player link play, each player with their own screen | **Co-op where every player has their own viewpoint and cover**: split screen on one machine, or AI Hub shared-world co-op for 1–4 players. |\\n107\\t| A B-movie secret-agent story: an island invaded, a villain's broadcasts, partner banter, bosses on vehicles | The Lumen Isles, the Black Current mercenary fleet, Admiral Krake's broadcasts on giant screens, CREST radio banter in gibberish voices with subtitles, bosses on a gunboat, in a lighthouse and in a crab mech. |\\n108\\t\\n109\\t**What we don't copy:** the agency (VSSE), the characters (Alan, Wesley, Alicia, Zott and the rest), the places\\n110\\t(Astigos, Lukano, Zagoria), the stage layouts, the music, the logos and the HUD art. The title is only a pun.\\n111\\t\\n112\\t### 1.2 Pillars\\n113\\t1. **Duck, pop, shoot.** Cover protects you and reloads you, but every second in cover costs TIME.\\n114\\t2. **Read the crisis.** Every attack that can hurt you is readable, by shape, sound and timing, never by color alone.\\n115\\t3. **Beat the clock.** Each area's TIME pushes you to expose yourself.\\n116\\t4. **Spectacle on rails.** Whip-pan arrivals, vehicle chases, explosions, breakable scenery and bosses that fill the\\n117\\t   screen.\\n118\\t5. **A B-movie heart.** Hammy heroes, a grandiose villain, radio banter.\\n119\\t6. **Every agent sees their own angle**, whether on a couch or online.\\n120\\t\\n121\\t### 1.3 Modes and the main menu\\n122\\t- **ARCADE**: one player plays Stages 1→3 with their cutscenes and the ending, and the high-score table is `arcade`.\\n123\\t- **CO-OP**: two players on one machine in split screen. P2 needs a gamepad. Same content as ARCADE; table `coop`.\\n124\\t- **STAGE SELECT**: any stage at any difficulty, with a table per stage. Every stage is open from the start: this is a\\n125\\t  demo, and every part should be reachable.\\n126\\t- **TRAINING**: the CREST shooting range, four drills that teach every mechanic (§5.8).\\n127\\t- **RECORDS**: high-score tables, the best Training time, and INTEL (the message bottles found).\\n128\\t- **SETTINGS**: the engine's screens plus our items (§4.15).\\n129\\t- **ABOUT**: credits, Source Information and the three feedback sections.\\n130\\t- **AI Hub session**: no title screen. The game opens on its own briefing card, then plays one stage as shared-world\\n131\\t  co-op for 1–4 players (§4.19).\\n132\\t\\n133\\t### 1.4 Length targets\\n134\\t| Part | Target |\\n135\\t|---|---|\\n136\\t| Training | 2–3 min |\\n137\\t| Stage 1 (4 scenes + boss) | about 6 min of play |\\n138\\t| Stage 2 (4 scenes + boss) | about 6.5 min |\\n139\\t| Stage 3 (4 scenes + two-phase final boss) | about 7 min |\\n140\\t| Cutscenes (skippable) | about 4 min in total |\\n141\\t| ARCADE run | 20–24 min |\\n142\\t| AI Hub round (one stage) | 6–7 min (`hub:session-minutes` = 7) |\\n143\\t\\n144\\t---\\n145\\t\\n146\\t## 2. Source information (verified at planning time)\\n147\\t\\n148\\tFetched from Wikipedia's \\\"Time Crisis 3\\\" article on 2026-11-03. Put these facts on the About screen and in the exit\\n149\\tinterview, and write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything not confirmed here or by the builder's own research.\\n150\\t\\n151\\t- **Developers:** Namco and Nextech. **Publisher:** Namco (arcade). The PS2 version was published by Namco Hometek in\\n152\\t  North America and Sony Computer Entertainment in Europe.\\n153\\t- **Arcade:** North America February 2003, Japan April 1, 2003, on **Namco System 246** hardware. Its title screen says\\n154\\t  2002, and the 2002/2003 dating differs between sources.\\n155\\t- **PlayStation 2:** North America October 21, 2003; Europe October 31, 2003; Japan November 20, 2003. It supports the\\n156\\t  GunCon 2, which has a button in place of the pedal, and two-player play, including two linked consoles and TVs.\\n157\\t- **Mechanics:**\\n158\\t  - the pedal for hiding and advancing;\\n159\\t  - weapon switching, new to the series;\\n160\\t  - four weapons: a 9-round handgun (the only one with unlimited ammo), a 200-round machine gun, a 50-shell shotgun\\n161\\t    and a 5-round grenade launcher with splash damage;\\n162\\t  - shooting yellow-clad soldiers refills the special weapons;\\n163\\t  - a refined crisis flash, in which life-threatening shots are brighter;\\n164\\t  - two-player link play.\\n165\\t- **PS2 extras:** a playable Alicia Winston, with sniper sections; the \\\"Crisis Mission\\\" exercise menu.\\n166\\t- **Setting:** six years after Time Crisis II, the Mediterranean island of Astigos is invaded by the Zagorian\\n167\\t  Federation under General Giorgio Zott. We mention this only on the About screen; none of it appears in the game.\\n168\\t- **Not confirmed:** the exact time-limit rules, the lives defaults, and how weapons are switched in the arcade\\n169\\t  version. Write \\\"unknown\\\" if you state them.\\n170\\t\\n171\\tValues for the Source Information block, for the builder to complete:\\n172\\t- **Date:** 2026-11-03 17:47:31\\n173\\t- **Source:** Time Crisis 3 (Namco/Nextech, 2003)\\n174\\t- **Plan:** `PLAN.md`\\n175\\t- **Genre and Perspective:** an on-rails light-gun shooter with a cover system, played from a first-person rail camera,\\n176\\t  with third-person cutscene cameras\\n177\\t- **Tech:** the my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 full engine (patched), a custom software 3D renderer module (\\\"Rail3D\\\"), and\\n178\\t  AI Hub session client 1.4.0. No other libraries.\\n179\\t- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-era pixel style, in perspective 3D\\n180\\t- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid procedural rigs, plus new weapons, poses and moves\\n181\\t- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth and tracker for music and SFX, plus a Web Audio formant gibberish voice synth\\n182\\t- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world co-op for 1–4 players\\n183\\t- **Testing:** headless Node tests (`game.advance` and `game.debug`), Playwright checks, the AI Hub harness\\n184\\t- **Research:** the engine handoff docs, the AI Hub protocol docs, the Wikipedia article (fetched 2026-11-03)\\n185\\t- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\\n186\\t- **Model** and **Usage:** as TASK.md says\\n187\\t\\n188\\t---\\n189\\t\\n190\\t## 3. Technology and architecture\\n191\\t\\n192\\t### 3.1 The decision: the handoff's 2D engine, plus our own perspective renderer module\\n193\\tTASK.md says to build on `dist/handoff`. The handoff holds the full 2D engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every\\n194\\tmodule), but not the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier). The engine guide's light-gun recipe leans on that 3D engine.\\n195\\tWe build a perspective first-person view on the 2D engine instead, because it gives us everything else:\\n196\\t- the exact fixed-step loop and headless `game.advance`;\\n197\\t- scenes, input with rebinding, pause and settings, the boot screen and audio;\\n198\\t- `E.Humanoid` rigs drawn by the engine from any view at any scale. `E.Mode7.view(k)` already draws rigs as\\n199\\t  billboards sized by distance.\\n200\\t- `E.shapeMesh(id)`, which gives any library shape (or one of ours) as triangles for \\\"a 3D renderer\\\";\\n201\\t- `E.materialTile(id, { band })`, which gives any material as a texture tile at any of the engine's five light bands.\\n202\\t\\n203\\tSo our module **Rail3D** draws the world itself:\\n204\\t- a z-buffered, perspective-correct, banded-light, fogged triangle rasterizer, run in JS into the game's own\\n205\\t  low-resolution buffer (320×240 minimum, `expand: true`, about 427×240 on 16:9);\\n206\\t- engine rigs composited into it as depth-tested billboards;\\n207\\t- our own 3D particles.\\n208\\t\\n209\\tThe result looks like a PS1 light-gun game drawn in the engine's own palette, shading and character art. It runs from\\n210\\t`file://`, bundles into one file, and the whole game logic stays testable headless.\\n211\\t\\n212\\tRejected: Mode 7 alone (a flat floor with billboards can't do rooms, stairs or cover that occludes properly); the 3D\\n213\\tengine plus three.js (it is not in the handoff, must be served rather than opened from disk, and sits outside the\\n214\\tnative pixel pipeline).\\n215\\t\\n216\\t### 3.2 Folder layout and commands\\n217\\t```\\n218\\tTideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731/\\n219\\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js        handoff v0.85.2 full engine, patched (§3.6)\\n220\\t  engine/PATCHES.md          every engine change, with its reason (it feeds Engine Feedback)\\n221\\t  hub/session.js             AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte (shipped)\\n222\\t  hub/harness.html bridge.js round.js   AI Hub harness (tests only, never shipped)\\n223\\t  src/                       the game, as fragments of one IIFE, concatenated in file-name order (table below)\\n224\\t  tests/run.mjs              THE test command; tests/*.test.mjs are its suites\\n225\\t  tools/build.mjs            concat → build/game.js; bundle → ../TideCrisis3-…-174731.html\\n226\\t  tools/hubcheck.mjs         serves hubtest/ on 127.0.0.1:0, runs the harness scenarios in Playwright, kills its own PID\\n227\\t  tools/check.mjs filmstrip.mjs   copied from the handoff (browser smoke test, motion contact sheets)\\n228\\t  index.html                 dev page: the engine + src/*.js loaded one by one (readable stack traces)\\n229\\t  package.json               \\\"type\\\": \\\"module\\\", devDependencies playwright 1.63.0\\n230\\t  .gitignore                 node_modules, .tmp, build, hubtest, check-output\\n231\\t```\\n232\\tCommands:\\n233\\t```\\n234\\tnode tools/build.mjs                      # build/game.js and the single HTML in the work folder root\\n235\\tnode tests/run.mjs                        # fast headless suites (the default)\\n236\\tnode tests/run.mjs weapons threats        # chosen suites only\\n237\\tnode tests/run.mjs --playthrough          # bot playthroughs of every stage and the whole ARCADE (slow)\\n238\\tnode tests/run.mjs --browser              # tools/check.mjs on the bundle at four window sizes\\n239\\tnode tests/run.mjs --hub                  # AI Hub harness scenarios in Chromium\\n240\\tnode tests/run.mjs --all\\n241\\t```\\n242\\t**Source fragments.** Each fragment starts with a header comment (what it owns, what it reads). Top-level `const`s\\n243\\tare shared because the build wraps everything in one `(() => { const E = My3D2dge; … })();`. The tests load the same\\n244\\t`build/game.js`. A fragment has no side effects except registering things; `90-main.js` starts the game.\\n245\\t\\n246\\t| File | Owns |\\n247\\t|---|---|\\n248\\t| `00-core.js` | `TC` namespace, units (`U = 16`), `def()` registries, event bus, RNG streams (`E.rng`), `TUNE` defaults (frozen) and live `tune`, difficulty tables |\\n249\\t| `01-math3d.js` | vec3 helpers, camera basis, project and unproject, aim↔ray, ray vs AABB/OBB/sphere/capsule, angle lerps, spring and trauma helpers |\\n250\\t| `02-materials.js` | new `E.MATERIALS`/`E.PATTERNS` entries, the texture bank (5 bands per material, lids), canvas textures (signs, live screens) |\\n251\\t| `03-geometry.js` | geometry builder `G`: brushes, prefabs, `E.shapeMesh` import, tessellation, static batching and baked light, colliders |\\n252\\t| `04-rail3d.js` | the renderer: framebuffer, depth, sky, triangle raster, billboards, splats, lines, decals, present |\\n253\\t| `05-fx.js` | 3D particles, tracers, explosions, muzzle flashes, decals, rain, splashes, fireworks, casings, screen FX |\\n254\\t| `10-input.js` | action map preset, per-player aim (mouse, pad, keys), aim assist, wheel, device tracking, prompt labels |\\n255\\t| `11-camera.js` | RailCam: rail following, slots, cover blend, carriers, shake, recoil, bob, cutscene camera, dev free camera |\\n256\\t| `12-weapons.js` | weapon registry and firing (hitscan, pellets, projectiles), ammo, switching |\\n257\\t| `13-player.js` | PlayerState: cover state, lives, i-frames, score, chain, stats, continues |\\n258\\t| `20-actors.js` | actor base: rig wrapper, hit volumes from joints, damage pipeline, reactions, deaths, corpses |\\n259\\t| `21-enemies.js` | enemy archetype registry and behavior state machines (cover points, entries, peeks, routes) |\\n260\\t| `22-threats.js` | ThreatDirector: telegraph, caps, minimum gap, pop-out protection, resolution, misses |\\n261\\t| `23-projectiles.js` | grenades, rockets, harpoons, torpedoes, missiles, flares, player GL rounds; all shootable |\\n262\\t| `24-objects.js` | breakables, explosive barrels, pickups, life rings, intel bottles, civilians, allies, doors |\\n263\\t| `25-bosses.js` | boss framework plus Stingray/Barracuda, Hammerhead, Scylla, Krake (also Bulldog, The Gull, Seawolf) |\\n264\\t| `30-stagefmt.js` | stage, scene and wave schema; the Director; validators |\\n265\\t| `31-stage1.js` … `33-stage3.js`, `34-range.js` | content: geometry, rails, scenes, waves, cues |\\n266\\t| `40-story.js` | speakers, cutscene scripts, radio lines, briefing pages |\\n267\\t| `41-voice.js` | gibberish voice synth |\\n268\\t| `42-audio.js` | songs, SFX voices, ambience, the spatial mix |\\n269\\t| `50-hud.js` | HUD |\\n270\\t| `51-screens.js` | title, menus, select, co-op join, briefing, results, continue, game over and initials, records, about, ending, how-to, touch-only notice |\\n271\\t| `52-dev.js` | dev mode panel, overlays, free camera |\\n272\\t| `53-attract.js` | attract controller and the Autopilot bot |\\n273\\t| `60-sim.js` | `Sim`: the authoritative state and `step(dt, commands)` |\\n274\\t| `61-local.js` | local session: drop-in co-op, split screen, routing devices to players |\\n275\\t| `62-net.js` | AI Hub: connect, card, host and replica replication, finish |\\n276\\t| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game`, scenes, boot steps, `game.debug` |\\n277\\t| `99-about.js` | About and credits data; `tools/build.mjs` also writes `exit-interview.json` from it |\\n278\\t\\n279\\t**The bundle.** `<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">` comes first. Next comes `hub/session.js`, the first script in `<head>`,\\n280\\tunmodified. Then the hub meta tags (§4.19), the viewport meta, the style, the engine's `E.bootSplash` markup at the\\n281\\tstart of `<body>`, `<canvas id=\\\"screen\\\">`, the engine script, and the game script. Nothing is fetched from the network.\\n282\\t\\n283\\t### 3.3 Units and conventions\\n284\\t- The engine's world: x east, y south, z up, **16 u = 1 m**. **Yaw** is in radians, with 0 = east (+x) and +π/2 = south\\n285\\t  (+y), the same as `E.angleTo` and `E.Path.at().angle`. **Pitch** is in radians, positive up. **FOV** is vertical,\\n286\\t  55° by default (dev slider 45–75°).\\n287\\t- The camera basis for yaw ψ and pitch θ:\\n288\\t  - forward `f = (cosθ cosψ, cosθ sinψ, sinθ)`;\\n289\\t  - right `r = (−sinψ, cosψ, 0)` (looking east, your right is south);\\n290\\t  - up `u = (−sinθ cosψ, −sinθ sinψ, cosθ)`.\\n291\\t\\n292\\t  View space is `x = d·r`, `y = d·u`, `z = d·f`. Screen: `sx = cx + x·F/z`, `sy = cy − y·F/z`, with\\n293\\t  `F = (h/2) / tan(fov/2)`. Roll rotates (x, y) before the divide.\\n294\\t- **Aim (view units):** the reticle is at `(ax, ay)`, with `sx = cx + ax·h/2` and `sy = cy − ay·h/2`. The ray\\n295\\t  direction in camera space is `normalize(ax·tan(fov/2), ay·tan(fov/2), 1)`. Headless, every viewport's aspect is\\n296\\t  16/9 unless the presentation layer sets it.\\n297\\t- **Human scale:** a Humanoid at `size: 1` is about 26 u tall (the asset library is \\\"sized for a 26-unit hero\\\").\\n298\\t  - Soldiers use `size: 1.0–1.1`. Measure the head-top height at boot (`rig.J.head` after one `rig.update`), store it in\\n299\\t    `TC.H.rig`, and have a test assert it is between 24 and 30 u.\\n300\\t  - Player eye height: **24 u standing, 12 u in cover.**\\n301\\t  - Crouch-height cover is 13–16 u (an engine crate is 15×15×14). High cover, which enemies peek around from the side,\\n302\\t    is 34–48 u.\\n303\\t  - A container is 98×39×42 u (6.1 × 2.4 × 2.6 m). A door is 16×34 u. A building floor is 48 u.\\n304\\t- Fights happen at 96–320 u (6–20 m), and bosses at up to 480 u. At FOV 55° and 240 px, a 26-u soldier at 10 m is\\n305\\t  about 37 px tall.\\n306\\t- **Time:** the Sim steps on the engine's `dt` (1/120 s × `timeScale`). All timers are in seconds of Sim time.\\n307\\t- **Randomness:** inside the Sim, only `sim.rng(stream)` (mulberry32 via `E.rng`, seeded from the run seed). Cosmetic\\n308\\t  FX may use their own stream. **No `Math.random` in `1x`–`3x`, `60` or `62` files**; a test greps for it.\\n309\\t\\n310\\t### 3.4 How it runs\\n311\\t**Engine scenes** (`game.start({ scene, scenes, boot })`):\\n312\\t- `title`\\n313\\t- `menu`\\n314\\t- `difficulty`\\n315\\t- `select`\\n316\\t- `coop`: join\\n317\\t- `briefing`\\n318\\t- `play`: every stage, the Training range and the cutscenes. It is pausable when not in a session.\\n319\\t- `results`\\n320\\t- `gameover`: initials\\n321\\t- `records`\\n322\\t- `about`\\n323\\t- `howto`\\n324\\t- `ending`: credits\\n325\\t- `attract`\\n326\\t- `session`: the AI Hub briefing card, then play\\n327\\t- `server`: an AI Hub host with no local player\\n328\\t\\n329\\tEvery scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }` (the wheel switches weapons) and `touch: []`.\\n330\\t\\n331\\t**`Sim`**\\n332\\t```js\\n333\\tconst sim = new TC.Sim({ mode: 'arcade' | 'stage' | 'coop' | 'range' | 'attract' | 'host' | 'replica',\\n334\\t  stage: 1, scene: '1-1', difficulty: 'normal', seed, cheats: { god, ammo, freeze, oneHit },\\n335\\t  players: [{ id: 0, name: 'P1', color: '#e8443a', agent: 'kai', aspect: 16 / 9 }] });\\n336\\tsim.step(dt, commands);   // commands[i] = { aim: [ax, ay], fireDown, firePressed, coverDown, coverPressed,\\n337\\t                          //                next, prev, select: 0..4 (0 = none), reload }\\n338\\tsim.state                 // plain data: phase, stage, scene, timer, players[], enemies[], projectiles[], objects[], boss\\n339\\tsim.events                // this step's events: { type: 'shot' | 'hit' | 'kill' | 'threat' | 'impact' | 'pickup' |\\n340\\t                          //   'spawn' | 'cue' | 'say' | 'phase' | 'clear' | 'timeup' | 'boss' | ..., ... }\\n341\\tsim.cameraFor(i)          // player i's logical camera pose {x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov} (no shake)\\n342\\tsim.jump(stage, sceneId, { phase })   // dev, attract, tests\\n343\\tsim.hash()                // determinism checks\\n344\\t```\\n345\\t`Sim` has its own coroutine runner with the same yield rules as `game.script`: a number of seconds, a predicate,\\n346\\tanother generator, nothing (one step). It does not use `game.script`, so several Sims can run in one Node process for\\n347\\tthe net tests, and a scene change never kills them.\\n348\\t\\n349\\t**One update step** (the `play` scene's `update(dt)`):\\n350\\t1. Build each player's command from its device (or the bot, or the network).\\n351\\t2. `sim.step(dt, cmds)`, which runs, in order:\\n352\\t   1. player commands, then the cover blend, reload and cooldowns, then shots;\\n353\\t   2. shot resolution (hitscan rays, pellets, launching projectiles);\\n354\\t   3. the Director (rail phase, waves by time, distance or clear; cues);\\n355\\t   4. enemies: their behaviors, then `rig.update`, then hit volumes rebuilt from the joints;\\n356\\t   5. ThreatDirector: new telegraphs, impacts resolved against cover;\\n357\\t   6. projectiles, objects, civilians, allies;\\n358\\t   7. bosses;\\n359\\t   8. TIME;\\n360\\t   9. events out.\\n361\\t3. The presentation consumes the events: SFX, voices, particles, HUD popups, camera trauma, rumble.\\n362\\t4. Online: `net.tick()` sends and applies messages (§4.19).\\n363\\t\\n364\\t**One frame** (`play.draw(r)`, once per viewport in split screen):\\n365\\t1. `Rail3D.begin(sim.cameraFor(i) + presentation offsets, viewport rect)`;\\n366\\t2. the sky;\\n367\\t3. static and dynamic meshes;\\n368\\t4. blob shadows;\\n369\\t5. actor billboards, far to near;\\n370\\t6. decals, particles, lines and glows;\\n371\\t7. screen FX (rain, cover vignette, hit edge);\\n372\\t8. `present(g)` through **one** `r.overlay`;\\n373\\t9. the HUD overlay for that viewport;\\n374\\t10. the dialog box.\\n375\\t\\n376\\tThe engine draws `game.flash()` after the scene's overlays, so the crisis flash and explosion flashes sit on top and\\n377\\tfollow the player's Screen flashes setting. In play, nothing goes through `r.actor`/`r.queue`, and\\n378\\t`game.lights.enabled = false`: Rail3D does its own lighting.\\n379\\t\\n380\\t### 3.5 Registries (the designer-facing data)\\n381\\t```js\\n382\\tdef(kind, id, spec)\\n383\\t```\\n384\\tThe kinds are `material`, `prefab`, `weapon`, `enemy`, `boss`, `stage`, `scene`, `speaker`, `voice`, `song`, `sfx`,\\n385\\t`cutscene`, `highlight` (attract playlist) and `intel`. Entries refer to each other by id. The validators (§4.10)\\n386\\tcheck every reference, so adding content never means touching a system.\\n387\\t\\n388\\t### 3.6 Engine patches (log each in `engine/PATCHES.md`)\\n389\\t1. **Long guns and off-hand items on `E.Humanoid`.**\\n390\\t   - New weapons: `weapon: 'rifle' | 'smg' | 'shotgun' | 'launcher' | 'sniper' | 'minigun' | 'harpoon' | 'cutlass' | 'flare'`.\\n391\\t   - New off-hand items: `offhand: 'shield' | 'hatch' | 'crate' | 'lantern' | null`.\\n392\\t   - Code to extend: the whitelist near `my-3d2dge.js` line 3960, `tip()` near 4011, the weapon rest direction near\\n393\\t     4231, and the weapon drawing in both the HD and the classic draw (the `// ---- weapons ----` blocks near 4535 and\\n394\\t     4616: `ops.push({ d, f })` with `Sp()`/`cap()`).\\n395\\t   - Two-handed weapons re-solve the left arm with `ik3` to a fore-grip at `handR + bladeDir · grip`.\\n396\\t   - `tip()` returns the muzzle.\\n397\\t   - `cutlass` is the sword path with a curved blade.\\n398\\t   - The shields block from the left forearm.\\n399\\t\\n400\\t   Acceptance: a filmstrip of every weapon idle, aiming (`point: true`, `aim` ±0.5) and firing, from the front, the side\\n401\\t   and three-quarter, with no detached hands.\\n402\\t2. **PlayStation glyphs by default.** In the STANDARD settings table (`glyphs`, about line 2999), the default becomes\\n403\\t   `'playstation'` and the options become `[['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']]`, as TASK.md asks.\\n404\\t3. **Mouse-wheel codes.** A `wheel` listener on the canvas presses `'WheelUp'` or `'WheelDown'` for one step;\\n405\\t   `codeLabel` gives \\\"Wheel up\\\" and \\\"Wheel down\\\". They can be bound and rebound like any code.\\n406\\t4. **Audio buses.** `game.audio.bus('sfx' | 'music')` returns the existing `_sfxBus` / `_musBus` GainNode, or null\\n407\\t   before sound starts. The voice synth and the richer SFX graphs connect there, so the volume settings apply to them.\\n408\\t5. (Optional, only if a test shows it's needed:) a hook that lets `E.Arcade.enter`'s letter wheel take its\\n409\\t   up/down/left/right from our input map.\\n410\\t\\n411\\tAlso confirm, with a test rather than a patch:\\n412\\t- `input.label()` gives `'R2'`/`'L2'` with PlayStation glyphs and `'RT'`/`'LT'` with Xbox glyphs;\\n413\\t- the codes on a custom preset include the menu keys. Copy `up down left right start pause confirm cancel` from\\n414\\t  `E.Input.DEFAULT` into our preset.\\n415\\t\\n416\\t---\\n417\\t\\n418\\t## 4. Systems\\n419\\t\\n420\\t### 4.1 Rail3D, the renderer\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"df29a640-59fb-4c49-8a39-745a7183bcd5\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:33:20.001Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84/work/PLAN.md\",\"content\":\"# Tide Crisis 3\\n\\n**The title.** As TASK.md asks, I brainstormed 50 riffs on *Time Crisis 3* without showing them, then picked one with an\\nOS-entropy RNG (`python3`, `secrets.randbelow(50)` → #9). In file names it is `TideCrisis3`, and the source is\\n`TimeCrisis3`.\\n\\n**The pitch.** Tide Crisis 3 is an original arcade light-gun shooter on rails, drawn in my-3D2dge's PS1-era pixel style\\nand played in first person. Two agents of C.R.E.S.T. (Covert Response & Emergency Strike Team) fight to take an island\\ncity's tidal barrier back from a disgraced admiral before the dawn king tide drowns the city. You take cover to dodge\\nand reload, then pop out to shoot. You switch between four weapons, read the \\\"crisis flash\\\" that marks every shot that\\ncan hit you, and beat the clock in every area. You can play alone, with two players on one machine (split screen), or\\nwith 1–4 players online in an AI Hub session.\\n\\n**The core loop** (write this sentence at the top of the game code): *the camera rides the rail to the next area (WAIT)\\n→ ACTION! starts the clock → pop out and shoot → duck when a crisis flash says a shot will hit → reload in cover →\\nclear the area before TIME runs out → ride on → beat the boss → STAGE CLEAR.*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Contents\\n0. Read this first: deliverables, rules, environment, glossary\\n1. The game: what we keep from the source, pillars, modes, length\\n2. Source information (verified at planning time)\\n3. Technology and architecture\\n4. Systems (each with its spec, numbers and tests)\\n5. Content: world, cast, tone, enemies, stages, bosses, script, art, animation, audio\\n6. Build order and milestones\\n7. Risks and how to de-risk them\\n8. Testing\\n9. Done checklist (every TASK.md requirement, and where this plan covers it)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Read this first\\n\\n### 0.1 Deliverables (exact names, all in this folder)\\n| What | Name |\\n|---|---|\\n| Dev folder (every dev file lives here) | `TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731/` |\\n| The game, one self-contained HTML file | `TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731.html` |\\n| Exit interview (exactly TASK.md's shape; check that it parses) | `exit-interview.json` |\\n\\nThe HTML inlines the patched engine, the game and the AI Hub session client. It plays from `file://` with no network.\\nSource Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in three places: the in-game About/Credits\\nscreen, `exit-interview.json`, and the final chat reply. Generate all three from one data file (`src/99-about.js`,\\n§4.14) so they agree.\\n\\n### 0.2 Golden rules\\n1. **Original world, original cast.** The source gives us a genre and a feel, nothing more (§1.1). Never use its names,\\n   levels, music, logos or UI art.\\n2. **The simulation knows nothing about rendering, and it is deterministic.** `Sim` holds the whole game state and\\n   steps it from player commands, with seeded RNG streams. The presentation layer (renderer, HUD, audio, FX) only reads\\n   it. Netcode, the headless tests, the bot and the attract mode all depend on this rule.\\n3. **Content is data, held in registries** (`def(kind, id, spec)`), and validators keep it honest (§4.10). Designers\\n   build the rest of the game later by adding entries, not by editing systems.\\n4. **No TODOs, placeholders or stubs.** If time runs short, cut whole scenes in the order in §6. Never cut a mechanic.\\n5. **Use the engine's standard features**: the pause menu, settings, control rebinding, full screen, the boot screen\\n   and auto-pause. Adjust them through `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`; don't rebuild them.\\n6. **Tests run behind one command** that prints only failures and a one-line count (§8). After a change, rerun only\\n   the suites that cover the code you changed.\\n7. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\\n\\n### 0.3 Environment notes\\n- Other agents share this machine. Read only what you need. Any server binds `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port\\n  (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it). Confirm the served page is your game before you trust a\\n  result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or a kill on whatever\\n  holds a port.\\n- **`/tmp` (a tmpfs) was 100% full at planning time.** `git clone` failed there with \\\"No space left on device\\\".\\n  Chromium and Playwright write their profiles to `TMPDIR`, so export `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp` (create it, and add it to\\n  `.gitignore`) before any browser tool runs.\\n- Playwright: the handoff pins `playwright` 1.63.0. A Chromium build (`chromium-1243`) is already in\\n  `~/.cache/ms-playwright`. If the versions don't match, run `npx playwright install chromium` with `TMPDIR` set.\\n- Put reference clones outside the shared `runs/` tree, as sparse checkouts in a scratch folder you own, e.g.\\n  `~/.cache/<run-id>/`. Take `dist/handoff` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge and `lib/hub` of\\n  github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub. Copy into the dev folder only the files you ship or run (§3.2). The handoff is 16 MB.\\n\\n### 0.4 Glossary\\n| Term | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| Stage | One of the three levels (plus the Training range), each with its own world geometry, palette, music and boss |\\n| Scene (area) | One fight point in a stage: a time limit, player slots, enemy cover points, waves, cues. Shown to players as \\\"AREA 2\\\" |\\n| Slot | Where player *i* stands in a scene: a *stand* pose (exposed) and a *cover* pose (hidden), each a camera pose |\\n| Cover point | A spot an enemy hides behind and peeks out from |\\n| Wave | A group of spawns that starts at a time, at a distance travelled, or when an earlier wave is cleared |\\n| Threat | An enemy attack that will hit a player who is still exposed when it lands. Telegraphed by the crisis flash |\\n| Director | The per-scene runner: waves, cues, the clear condition |\\n| Rail | The camera paths between scenes (`E.Path`), and inside moving scenes |\\n| Carrier | A moving object a slot rides on (the boat, the funicular car, the tower lift) |\\n| View units (vu) | Resolution-free screen coordinates: `ax ∈ [−A, A]`, `ay ∈ [−1, 1]`, y up, where A is the viewport's aspect ratio |\\n| u | World units. 16 u = 1 m |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The game\\n\\n### 1.1 From the source into our game\\n| Distinctive in Time Crisis 3 (and the series) | What we build (original) |\\n|---|---|\\n| A foot pedal: release it to hide behind cover and reload, press it to stand and shoot | **Cover is the game.** Hold L2, right mouse button or Space to duck behind real geometry; let go to pop out. You are invulnerable in cover, and you can only reload there. Toggle mode is a setting. |\\n| The series' first weapon-select system: a 9-round handgun with unlimited ammo, a machine gun, a shotgun, a grenade launcher | The same four weapon roles with our own names and numbers (§4.4). Fire while hidden cycles weapons, so a player with only a trigger can switch; R1/L1, the mouse wheel and the 1–4 keys also switch. |\\n| Shooting yellow-clad soldiers refills special-weapon ammo | **Quartermasters in orange life vests** carry ammo crates. Kill one and you get special ammo at once. |\\n| The crisis flash: shots that will hit you flash brighter, so you know to duck | **Crisis flash**: every threat shows a shrinking red ring with a white core and a \\\"!\\\" glyph at the shooter's muzzle, a muzzle flare, a rising tick sound, and an arrow at the screen edge if the shooter is off screen. Only threats can hurt you; misses are spectacle. |\\n| Red-uniformed soldiers (series tradition) who shoot fast and accurately | **Red Tide elites**: crimson coats, short telegraphs, every shot a threat. |\\n| A time limit on every area | **TIME** on every scene, drawn as a rising-tide gauge. When it runs out (\\\"TIDE'S IN!\\\") you lose a life. |\\n| Lives, continues, arcade score | Lives, 10-second continues, score, a chain multiplier, ranks, high-score initials. |\\n| Two-player link play, each player with their own screen | **Co-op where every player has their own viewpoint and cover**: split screen on one machine, or AI Hub shared-world co-op for 1–4 players. |\\n| A B-movie secret-agent story: an island invaded, a villain's broadcasts, partner banter, bosses on vehicles | The Lumen Isles, the Black Current mercenary fleet, Admiral Krake's broadcasts on giant screens, CREST radio banter in gibberish voices with subtitles, bosses on a gunboat, in a lighthouse and in a crab mech. |\\n\\n**What we don't copy:** the agency (VSSE), the characters (Alan, Wesley, Alicia, Zott and the rest), the places\\n(Astigos, Lukano, Zagoria), the stage layouts, the music, the logos and the HUD art. The title is only a pun.\\n\\n### 1.2 Pillars\\n1. **Duck, pop, shoot.** Cover protects you and reloads you, but every second in cover costs TIME.\\n2. **Read the crisis.** Every attack that can hurt you is readable, by shape, sound and timing, never by color alone.\\n3. **Beat the clock.** Each area's TIME pushes you to expose yourself.\\n4. **Spectacle on rails.** Whip-pan arrivals, vehicle chases, explosions, breakable scenery and bosses that fill the\\n   screen.\\n5. **A B-movie heart.** Hammy heroes, a grandiose villain, radio banter.\\n6. **Every agent sees their own angle**, whether on a couch or online.\\n\\n### 1.3 Modes and the main menu\\n- **ARCADE**: one player plays Stages 1→3 with their cutscenes and the ending, and the high-score table is `arcade`.\\n- **CO-OP**: two players on one machine in split screen. P2 needs a gamepad. Same content as ARCADE; table `coop`.\\n- **STAGE SELECT**: any stage at any difficulty, with a table per stage. Every stage is open from the start: this is a\\n  demo, and every part should be reachable.\\n- **TRAINING**: the CREST shooting range, four drills that teach every mechanic (§5.8).\\n- **RECORDS**: high-score tables, the best Training time, and INTEL (the message bottles found).\\n- **SETTINGS**: the engine's screens plus our items (§4.15).\\n- **ABOUT**: credits, Source Information and the three feedback sections.\\n- **AI Hub session**: no title screen. The game opens on its own briefing card, then plays one stage as shared-world\\n  co-op for 1–4 players (§4.19).\\n\\n### 1.4 Length targets\\n| Part | Target |\\n|---|---|\\n| Training | 2–3 min |\\n| Stage 1 (4 scenes + boss) | about 6 min of play |\\n| Stage 2 (4 scenes + boss) | about 6.5 min |\\n| Stage 3 (4 scenes + two-phase final boss) | about 7 min |\\n| Cutscenes (skippable) | about 4 min in total |\\n| ARCADE run | 20–24 min |\\n| AI Hub round (one stage) | 6–7 min (`hub:session-minutes` = 7) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Source information (verified at planning time)\\n\\nFetched from Wikipedia's \\\"Time Crisis 3\\\" article on 2026-11-03. Put these facts on the About screen and in the exit\\ninterview, and write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything not confirmed here or by the builder's own research.\\n\\n- **Developers:** Namco and Nextech. **Publisher:** Namco (arcade). The PS2 version was published by Namco Hometek in\\n  North America and Sony Computer Entertainment in Europe.\\n- **Arcade:** North America February 2003, Japan April 1, 2003, on **Namco System 246** hardware. Its title screen says\\n  2002, and the 2002/2003 dating differs between sources.\\n- **PlayStation 2:** North America October 21, 2003; Europe October 31, 2003; Japan November 20, 2003. It supports the\\n  GunCon 2, which has a button in place of the pedal, and two-player play, including two linked consoles and TVs.\\n- **Mechanics:**\\n  - the pedal for hiding and advancing;\\n  - weapon switching, new to the series;\\n  - four weapons: a 9-round handgun (the only one with unlimited ammo), a 200-round machine gun, a 50-shell shotgun\\n    and a 5-round grenade launcher with splash damage;\\n  - shooting yellow-clad soldiers refills the special weapons;\\n  - a refined crisis flash, in which life-threatening shots are brighter;\\n  - two-player link play.\\n- **PS2 extras:** a playable Alicia Winston, with sniper sections; the \\\"Crisis Mission\\\" exercise menu.\\n- **Setting:** six years after Time Crisis II, the Mediterranean island of Astigos is invaded by the Zagorian\\n  Federation under General Giorgio Zott. We mention this only on the About screen; none of it appears in the game.\\n- **Not confirmed:** the exact time-limit rules, the lives defaults, and how weapons are switched in the arcade\\n  version. Write \\\"unknown\\\" if you state them.\\n\\nValues for the Source Information block, for the builder to complete:\\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 17:47:31\\n- **Source:** Time Crisis 3 (Namco/Nextech, 2003)\\n- **Plan:** `PLAN.md`\\n- **Genre and Perspective:** an on-rails light-gun shooter with a cover system, played from a first-person rail camera,\\n  with third-person cutscene cameras\\n- **Tech:** the my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 full engine (patched), a custom software 3D renderer module (\\\"Rail3D\\\"), and\\n  AI Hub session client 1.4.0. No other libraries.\\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-era pixel style, in perspective 3D\\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid procedural rigs, plus new weapons, poses and moves\\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth and tracker for music and SFX, plus a Web Audio formant gibberish voice synth\\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world co-op for 1–4 players\\n- **Testing:** headless Node tests (`game.advance` and `game.debug`), Playwright checks, the AI Hub harness\\n- **Research:** the engine handoff docs, the AI Hub protocol docs, the Wikipedia article (fetched 2026-11-03)\\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\\n- **Model** and **Usage:** as TASK.md says\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Technology and architecture\\n\\n### 3.1 The decision: the handoff's 2D engine, plus our own perspective renderer module\\nTASK.md says to build on `dist/handoff`. The handoff holds the full 2D engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every\\nmodule), but not the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier). The engine guide's light-gun recipe leans on that 3D engine.\\nWe build a perspective first-person view on the 2D engine instead, because it gives us everything else:\\n- the exact fixed-step loop and headless `game.advance`;\\n- scenes, input with rebinding, pause and settings, the boot screen and audio;\\n- `E.Humanoid` rigs drawn by the engine from any view at any scale. `E.Mode7.view(k)` already draws rigs as\\n  billboards sized by distance.\\n- `E.shapeMesh(id)`, which gives any library shape (or one of ours) as triangles for \\\"a 3D renderer\\\";\\n- `E.materialTile(id, { band })`, which gives any material as a texture tile at any of the engine's five light bands.\\n\\nSo our module **Rail3D** draws the world itself:\\n- a z-buffered, perspective-correct, banded-light, fogged triangle rasterizer, run in JS into the game's own\\n  low-resolution buffer (320×240 minimum, `expand: true`, about 427×240 on 16:9);\\n- engine rigs composited into it as depth-tested billboards;\\n- our own 3D particles.\\n\\nThe result looks like a PS1 light-gun game drawn in the engine's own palette, shading and character art. It runs from\\n`file://`, bundles into one file, and the whole game logic stays testable headless.\\n\\nRejected: Mode 7 alone (a flat floor with billboards can't do rooms, stairs or cover that occludes properly); the 3D\\nengine plus three.js (it is not in the handoff, must be served rather than opened from disk, and sits outside the\\nnative pixel pipeline).\\n\\n### 3.2 Folder layout and commands\\n```\\nTideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731/\\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js        handoff v0.85.2 full engine, patched (§3.6)\\n  engine/PATCHES.md          every engine change, with its reason (it feeds Engine Feedback)\\n  hub/session.js             AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte (shipped)\\n  hub/harness.html bridge.js round.js   AI Hub harness (tests only, never shipped)\\n  src/                       the game, as fragments of one IIFE, concatenated in file-name order (table below)\\n  tests/run.mjs              THE test command; tests/*.test.mjs are its suites\\n  tools/build.mjs            concat → build/game.js; bundle → ../TideCrisis3-…-174731.html\\n  tools/hubcheck.mjs         serves hubtest/ on 127.0.0.1:0, runs the harness scenarios in Playwright, kills its own PID\\n  tools/check.mjs filmstrip.mjs   copied from the handoff (browser smoke test, motion contact sheets)\\n  index.html                 dev page: the engine + src/*.js loaded one by one (readable stack traces)\\n  package.json               \\\"type\\\": \\\"module\\\", devDependencies playwright 1.63.0\\n  .gitignore                 node_modules, .tmp, build, hubtest, check-output\\n```\\nCommands:\\n```\\nnode tools/build.mjs                      # build/game.js and the single HTML in the work folder root\\nnode tests/run.mjs                        # fast headless suites (the default)\\nnode tests/run.mjs weapons threats        # chosen suites only\\nnode tests/run.mjs --playthrough          # bot playthroughs of every stage and the whole ARCADE (slow)\\nnode tests/run.mjs --browser              # tools/check.mjs on the bundle at four window sizes\\nnode tests/run.mjs --hub                  # AI Hub harness scenarios in Chromium\\nnode tests/run.mjs --all\\n```\\n**Source fragments.** Each fragment starts with a header comment (what it owns, what it reads). Top-level `const`s\\nare shared because the build wraps everything in one `(() => { const E = My3D2dge; … })();`. The tests load the same\\n`build/game.js`. A fragment has no side effects except registering things; `90-main.js` starts the game.\\n\\n| File | Owns |\\n|---|---|\\n| `00-core.js` | `TC` namespace, units (`U = 16`), `def()` registries, event bus, RNG streams (`E.rng`), `TUNE` defaults (frozen) and live `tune`, difficulty tables |\\n| `01-math3d.js` | vec3 helpers, camera basis, project and unproject, aim↔ray, ray vs AABB/OBB/sphere/capsule, angle lerps, spring and trauma helpers |\\n| `02-materials.js` | new `E.MATERIALS`/`E.PATTERNS` entries, the texture bank (5 bands per material, lids), canvas textures (signs, live screens) |\\n| `03-geometry.js` | geometry builder `G`: brushes, prefabs, `E.shapeMesh` import, tessellation, static batching and baked light, colliders |\\n| `04-rail3d.js` | the renderer: framebuffer, depth, sky, triangle raster, billboards, splats, lines, decals, present |\\n| `05-fx.js` | 3D particles, tracers, explosions, muzzle flashes, decals, rain, splashes, fireworks, casings, screen FX |\\n| `10-input.js` | action map preset, per-player aim (mouse, pad, keys), aim assist, wheel, device tracking, prompt labels |\\n| `11-camera.js` | RailCam: rail following, slots, cover blend, carriers, shake, recoil, bob, cutscene camera, dev free camera |\\n| `12-weapons.js` | weapon registry and firing (hitscan, pellets, projectiles), ammo, switching |\\n| `13-player.js` | PlayerState: cover state, lives, i-frames, score, chain, stats, continues |\\n| `20-actors.js` | actor base: rig wrapper, hit volumes from joints, damage pipeline, reactions, deaths, corpses |\\n| `21-enemies.js` | enemy archetype registry and behavior state machines (cover points, entries, peeks, routes) |\\n| `22-threats.js` | ThreatDirector: telegraph, caps, minimum gap, pop-out protection, resolution, misses |\\n| `23-projectiles.js` | grenades, rockets, harpoons, torpedoes, missiles, flares, player GL rounds; all shootable |\\n| `24-objects.js` | breakables, explosive barrels, pickups, life rings, intel bottles, civilians, allies, doors |\\n| `25-bosses.js` | boss framework plus Stingray/Barracuda, Hammerhead, Scylla, Krake (also Bulldog, The Gull, Seawolf) |\\n| `30-stagefmt.js` | stage, scene and wave schema; the Director; validators |\\n| `31-stage1.js` … `33-stage3.js`, `34-range.js` | content: geometry, rails, scenes, waves, cues |\\n| `40-story.js` | speakers, cutscene scripts, radio lines, briefing pages |\\n| `41-voice.js` | gibberish voice synth |\\n| `42-audio.js` | songs, SFX voices, ambience, the spatial mix |\\n| `50-hud.js` | HUD |\\n| `51-screens.js` | title, menus, select, co-op join, briefing, results, continue, game over and initials, records, about, ending, how-to, touch-only notice |\\n| `52-dev.js` | dev mode panel, overlays, free camera |\\n| `53-attract.js` | attract controller and the Autopilot bot |\\n| `60-sim.js` | `Sim`: the authoritative state and `step(dt, commands)` |\\n| `61-local.js` | local session: drop-in co-op, split screen, routing devices to players |\\n| `62-net.js` | AI Hub: connect, card, host and replica replication, finish |\\n| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game`, scenes, boot steps, `game.debug` |\\n| `99-about.js` | About and credits data; `tools/build.mjs` also writes `exit-interview.json` from it |\\n\\n**The bundle.** `<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">` comes first. Next comes `hub/session.js`, the first script in `<head>`,\\nunmodified. Then the hub meta tags (§4.19), the viewport meta, the style, the engine's `E.bootSplash` markup at the\\nstart of `<body>`, `<canvas id=\\\"screen\\\">`, the engine script, and the game script. Nothing is fetched from the network.\\n\\n### 3.3 Units and conventions\\n- The engine's world: x east, y south, z up, **16 u = 1 m**. **Yaw** is in radians, with 0 = east (+x) and +π/2 = south\\n  (+y), the same as `E.angleTo` and `E.Path.at().angle`. **Pitch** is in radians, positive up. **FOV** is vertical,\\n  55° by default (dev slider 45–75°).\\n- The camera basis for yaw ψ and pitch θ:\\n  - forward `f = (cosθ cosψ, cosθ sinψ, sinθ)`;\\n  - right `r = (−sinψ, cosψ, 0)` (looking east, your right is south);\\n  - up `u = (−sinθ cosψ, −sinθ sinψ, cosθ)`.\\n\\n  View space is `x = d·r`, `y = d·u`, `z = d·f`. Screen: `sx = cx + x·F/z`, `sy = cy − y·F/z`, with\\n  `F = (h/2) / tan(fov/2)`. Roll rotates (x, y) before the divide.\\n- **Aim (view units):** the reticle is at `(ax, ay)`, with `sx = cx + ax·h/2` and `sy = cy − ay·h/2`. The ray\\n  direction in camera space is `normalize(ax·tan(fov/2), ay·tan(fov/2), 1)`. Headless, every viewport's aspect is\\n  16/9 unless the presentation layer sets it.\\n- **Human scale:** a Humanoid at `size: 1` is about 26 u tall (the asset library is \\\"sized for a 26-unit hero\\\").\\n  - Soldiers use `size: 1.0–1.1`. Measure the head-top height at boot (`rig.J.head` after one `rig.update`), store it in\\n    `TC.H.rig`, and have a test assert it is between 24 and 30 u.\\n  - Player eye height: **24 u standing, 12 u in cover.**\\n  - Crouch-height cover is 13–16 u (an engine crate is 15×15×14). High cover, which enemies peek around from the side,\\n    is 34–48 u.\\n  - A container is 98×39×42 u (6.1 × 2.4 × 2.6 m). A door is 16×34 u. A building floor is 48 u.\\n- Fights happen at 96–320 u (6–20 m), and bosses at up to 480 u. At FOV 55° and 240 px, a 26-u soldier at 10 m is\\n  about 37 px tall.\\n- **Time:** the Sim steps on the engine's `dt` (1/120 s × `timeScale`). All timers are in seconds of Sim time.\\n- **Randomness:** inside the Sim, only `sim.rng(stream)` (mulberry32 via `E.rng`, seeded from the run seed). Cosmetic\\n  FX may use their own stream. **No `Math.random` in `1x`–`3x`, `60` or `62` files**; a test greps for it.\\n\\n### 3.4 How it runs\\n**Engine scenes** (`game.start({ scene, scenes, boot })`):\\n- `title`\\n- `menu`\\n- `difficulty`\\n- `select`\\n- `coop`: join\\n- `briefing`\\n- `play`: every stage, the Training range and the cutscenes. It is pausable when not in a session.\\n- `results`\\n- `gameover`: initials\\n- `records`\\n- `about`\\n- `howto`\\n- `ending`: credits\\n- `attract`\\n- `session`: the AI Hub briefing card, then play\\n- `server`: an AI Hub host with no local player\\n\\nEvery scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }` (the wheel switches weapons) and `touch: []`.\\n\\n**`Sim`**\\n```js\\nconst sim = new TC.Sim({ mode: 'arcade' | 'stage' | 'coop' | 'range' | 'attract' | 'host' | 'replica',\\n  stage: 1, scene: '1-1', difficulty: 'normal', seed, cheats: { god, ammo, freeze, oneHit },\\n  players: [{ id: 0, name: 'P1', color: '#e8443a', agent: 'kai', aspect: 16 / 9 }] });\\nsim.step(dt, commands);   // commands[i] = { aim: [ax, ay], fireDown, firePressed, coverDown, coverPressed,\\n                          //                next, prev, select: 0..4 (0 = none), reload }\\nsim.state                 // plain data: phase, stage, scene, timer, players[], enemies[], projectiles[], objects[], boss\\nsim.events                // this step's events: { type: 'shot' | 'hit' | 'kill' | 'threat' | 'impact' | 'pickup' |\\n                          //   'spawn' | 'cue' | 'say' | 'phase' | 'clear' | 'timeup' | 'boss' | ..., ... }\\nsim.cameraFor(i)          // player i's logical camera pose {x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov} (no shake)\\nsim.jump(stage, sceneId, { phase })   // dev, attract, tests\\nsim.hash()                // determinism checks\\n```\\n`Sim` has its own coroutine runner with the same yield rules as `game.script`: a number of seconds, a predicate,\\nanother generator, nothing (one step). It does not use `game.script`, so several Sims can run in one Node process for\\nthe net tests, and a scene change never kills them.\\n\\n**One update step** (the `play` scene's `update(dt)`):\\n1. Build each player's command from its device (or the bot, or the network).\\n2. `sim.step(dt, cmds)`, which runs, in order:\\n   1. player commands, then the cover blend, reload and cooldowns, then shots;\\n   2. shot resolution (hitscan rays, pellets, launching projectiles);\\n   3. the Director (rail phase, waves by time, distance or clear; cues);\\n   4. enemies: their behaviors, then `rig.update`, then hit volumes rebuilt from the joints;\\n   5. ThreatDirector: new telegraphs, impacts resolved against cover;\\n   6. projectiles, objects, civilians, allies;\\n   7. bosses;\\n   8. TIME;\\n   9. events out.\\n3. The presentation consumes the events: SFX, voices, particles, HUD popups, camera trauma, rumble.\\n4. Online: `net.tick()` sends and applies messages (§4.19).\\n\\n**One frame** (`play.draw(r)`, once per viewport in split screen):\\n1. `Rail3D.begin(sim.cameraFor(i) + presentation offsets, viewport rect)`;\\n2. the sky;\\n3. static and dynamic meshes;\\n4. blob shadows;\\n5. actor billboards, far to near;\\n6. decals, particles, lines and glows;\\n7. screen FX (rain, cover vignette, hit edge);\\n8. `present(g)` through **one** `r.overlay`;\\n9. the HUD overlay for that viewport;\\n10. the dialog box.\\n\\nThe engine draws `game.flash()` after the scene's overlays, so the crisis flash and explosion flashes sit on top and\\nfollow the player's Screen flashes setting. In play, nothing goes through `r.actor`/`r.queue`, and\\n`game.lights.enabled = false`: Rail3D does its own lighting.\\n\\n### 3.5 Registries (the designer-facing data)\\n```js\\ndef(kind, id, spec)\\n```\\nThe kinds are `material`, `prefab`, `weapon`, `enemy`, `boss`, `stage`, `scene`, `speaker`, `voice`, `song`, `sfx`,\\n`cutscene`, `highlight` (attract playlist) and `intel`. Entries refer to each other by id. The validators (§4.10)\\ncheck every reference, so adding content never means touching a system.\\n\\n### 3.6 Engine patches (log each in `engine/PATCHES.md`)\\n1. **Long guns and off-hand items on `E.Humanoid`.**\\n   - New weapons: `weapon: 'rifle' | 'smg' | 'shotgun' | 'launcher' | 'sniper' | 'minigun' | 'harpoon' | 'cutlass' | 'flare'`.\\n   - New off-hand items: `offhand: 'shield' | 'hatch' | 'crate' | 'lantern' | null`.\\n   - Code to extend: the whitelist near `my-3d2dge.js` line 3960, `tip()` near 4011, the weapon rest direction near\\n     4231, and the weapon drawing in both the HD and the classic draw (the `// ---- weapons ----` blocks near 4535 and\\n     4616: `ops.push({ d, f })` with `Sp()`/`cap()`).\\n   - Two-handed weapons re-solve the left arm with `ik3` to a fore-grip at `handR + bladeDir · grip`.\\n   - `tip()` returns the muzzle.\\n   - `cutlass` is the sword path with a curved blade.\\n   - The shields block from the left forearm.\\n\\n   Acceptance: a filmstrip of every weapon idle, aiming (`point: true`, `aim` ±0.5) and firing, from the front, the side\\n   and three-quarter, with no detached hands.\\n2. **PlayStation glyphs by default.** In the STANDARD settings table (`glyphs`, about line 2999), the default becomes\\n   `'playstation'` and the options become `[['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']]`, as TASK.md asks.\\n3. **Mouse-wheel codes.** A `wheel` listener on the canvas presses `'WheelUp'` or `'WheelDown'` for one step;\\n   `codeLabel` gives \\\"Wheel up\\\" and \\\"Wheel down\\\". They can be bound and rebound like any code.\\n4. **Audio buses.** `game.audio.bus('sfx' | 'music')` returns the existing `_sfxBus` / `_musBus` GainNode, or null\\n   before sound starts. The voice synth and the richer SFX graphs connect there, so the volume settings apply to them.\\n5. (Optional, only if a test shows it's needed:) a hook that lets `E.Arcade.enter`'s letter wheel take its\\n   up/down/left/right from our input map.\\n\\nAlso confirm, with a test rather than a patch:\\n- `input.label()` gives `'R2'`/`'L2'` with PlayStation glyphs and `'RT'`/`'LT'` with Xbox glyphs;\\n- the codes on a custom preset include the menu keys. Copy `up down left right start pause confirm cancel` from\\n  `E.Input.DEFAULT` into our preset.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Systems\\n\\n### 4.1 Rail3D, the renderer\",\"numLines\":420,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":1671}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_0179m9mzcfnVZZDrAfrT3bfc\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"d0973d4e-e401-40fc-bbaf-c84069b8a714\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":250,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":200,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"e2590f6d-d54b-4195-a451-1ad7b40ecbab\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":500,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":250,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"1d848d33-d326-467b-b595-cd967c06a69d\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174743__x8534bd/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1711,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1711,"newLines":5,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01K9uqak3TQyZtxkc1hmUede\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"engine\\nhub\\nscripts\\nsparsetest\\n5.5G\\t/home/micha/.cache/plan-x8534bd-wzXO\\nremoved /home/micha/.cache/plan-x8534bd-wzXO\\nno scratch dirs left\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"8a4f3ee9-c6c3-4444-952d-fed941ec0208\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:33:13.421Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"engine\\nhub\\nscripts\\nsparsetest\\n5.5G\\t/home/micha/.cache/plan-x8534bd-wzXO\\nremoved /home/micha/.cache/plan-x8534bd-wzXO\\nno scratch dirs left\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false,\"bashEditDiff\":{\"files\":[],\"moreFiles\":0,\"unavailable\":true}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01K9uqak3TQyZtxkc1hmUede\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"7181f867-74b0-48bb-a5dd-0cb04e6b8ea5\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":150,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"94dcef28-df30-41d6-9dc8-f2d78e6aaab3\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":273,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":123,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"1392591c-bd8b-4a89-a620-4544819ef0bd\"}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2Van93M3F5V12QTv3v\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":602,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":496254,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":602},\"output_tokens\":5,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\",\"uuid\":\"7deb89a0-c23e-4240-a073-3f32a3c2dd19\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:33:22.885Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2VaX1cREdyDLaXw8yy\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":8652,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174755__x531263/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2060,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2060,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":126850,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"f93111a9-5060-4eb2-b5bf-6a1585c61e13\",\"uuid\":\"f3dc27c4-9fb7-433b-a1e2-96edd2ddcb4b\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":127050,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":200,\"session_id\":\"f93111a9-5060-4eb2-b5bf-6a1585c61e13\",\"uuid\":\"3ffa6088-b054-4393-a953-9dba520ae2ee\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":127250,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":200,\"session_id\":\"f93111a9-5060-4eb2-b5bf-6a1585c61e13\",\"uuid\":\"24cef739-00d1-429d-805b-75e956984535\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":127450,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":200,\"session_id\":\"f93111a9-5060-4eb2-b5bf-6a1585c61e13\",\"uuid\":\"42c17967-b900-4732-8bf9-9263e1bbd006\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1724,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1724,"newLines":5,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_019HbdVWVqfcrMmcpoW8ydKr\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"339\\t**Camera.** `{ s, x, h, dir: 1 | -1, fov: 75, horizon: .40, lookX, lookY, pitchPx, rollPx, shake }`.\\n340\\t\\n341\\t**Projection.** Keep road.js's two passes (near→far to accumulate the bend and clip at crests, then far→near to paint), but use **square pixels**:\\n342\\t\\n343\\t```\\n344\\tdepth = 1 / tan(fov/2)\\n345\\tscale = depth / d                    // d = distance ahead of the camera (m), must be > 0.5\\n346\\tsx = cx + scale * (wx - camX) * hw   // hw = W/2\\n347\\tsy = cy - scale * (wy - camY) * hw   // NOTE: hw again. road.js uses hh here, which squashes the world on wide screens\\n348\\tcx = vp.x + W/2 + lookX ;  cy = vp.y + H*horizon + lookY + pitchPx\\n349\\t```\\n350\\t- `wx = segment bend + lateral offset`; `wy = segment height + object height`.\\n351\\t- Pixels per metre at a point: `k = scale * hw`.\\n352\\t- Use horizon 0.40 for the cab (the dash covers the bottom 30%) and 0.50 for full-screen exterior cameras.\\n353\\t- Draw distance is 360 segments (1.8 km). The fog default fades from 900 m.\\n354\\t\\n355\\t**Rear view (`dir = -1`).** Walk the segments backward, negate the curve and every lateral offset, and swap the strip ends. This is the forward view\\n356\\tof the mirrored line, so the same painter works. It is used by the platform, trackside and Stop-in cameras (§4.13).\\n357\\t\\n358\\t**Painting, per segment, far→near:**\\n359\\t1. **Ground band.**\\n360\\t   - The full-width ground row is the zone's ground colour, alternating two tones every 4 segments for a sense of speed.\\n361\\t   - On **bridges**, draw water instead: a band 8 m below the deck, with animated glints.\\n362\\t   - At **crossings**, draw an asphalt road band across all x, with white edge lines.\\n363\\t2. **Track beds.** For each track, a ballast quad 3.0 m wide. Side tracks and sidings are darker and rustier.\\n364\\t3. **Sleepers.** Only within 160 m of the camera. Split each segment into 8 slices and draw one dark quad per sleeper (0.24 m along s, 2.4 m\\n365\\t   across). Beyond 160 m the ballast tone stands in.\\n366\\t4. **Rails.**\\n367\\t   - Two quads at ±0.533 m from the track centre, 0.065 m wide, in steel grey.\\n368\\t   - Where the rail is 2 px or wider, add a 1-px bright top line.\\n369\\t   - When wet, the rails get brighter and pick up the sky colour.\\n370\\t5. **Strips** (below): sorted by lateral distance from the camera, farthest first.\\n371\\t6. **Things** (below): also sorted farthest lateral first.\\n372\\t7. **Train slices**: actors are drawn as strips (§4.4), plus their front-face thing.\\n373\\t8. **Catenary wires.**\\n374\\t   - For each electrified track, two 1-px lines between this segment's projected points: the contact wire at y +5.1 and the messenger at +5.9.\\n375\\t   - The wire zig-zags ±0.2 m, alternating per mast. The converging wires are a strong depth cue; keep them.\\n376\\t\\n377\\t**Strips** are extrusions along the line: `{ s0, s1, x0, x1, y0, y1, faces: { side, top, under, front, back }, kind }`.\\n378\\t- Each face has a drawer `(g, quad, u0, u1, k, tint)`, where u is metres along s, so patterns repeat in world space (windows every 2.5 m, doors\\n379\\t  every 5 m, a tactile line).\\n380\\t- Which faces show:\\n381\\t  - the **side** nearer to camX (x1 if the strip lies left of the camera, x0 if right; none if it spans the camera);\\n382\\t  - **top** if the camera is above y1;\\n383\\t  - **under** if the camera is below y0 (canopies, overpasses, station roofs);\\n384\\t  - **front** or back where s0/s1 falls inside the slice being painted.\\n385\\t- Strips are used for:\\n386\\t  - platforms: y 0→1.1 m; top face with a white edge line and a yellow tactile strip 0.8 m in;\\n387\\t  - canopies (y 3.6→3.9), station buildings, stair housings;\\n388\\t  - sound walls, fences, retaining walls;\\n389\\t  - truss sides (X pattern);\\n390\\t  - tunnel walls and roof (road.js's tunnel, generalised; roof at 6.2 m; portal face; a wall lamp every 25 m);\\n391\\t  - overpasses: cross strips spanning all x, with an under face;\\n392\\t  - rows of houses and warehouses, viaduct parapets;\\n393\\t  - **trains** (§4.4).\\n394\\t\\n395\\t**Things** are point objects: `{ s, x, y, w, h, draw(g, sx, sy, k, ctx) }`.\\n396\\t- `(sx, sy)` is the screen position of the base centre; `k` is pixels per metre.\\n397\\t- Every drawer picks a level of detail from k: below 0.4, a dot or nothing; below 3, a 2–3 tone silhouette; 3 and up, full detail with\\n398\\t  `E.tones` shading.\\n399\\t- `E.sprite` sprites and `E.shapeSprite` bakes are wrapped as things with metres-per-pixel.\\n400\\t- **Humanoids and Blobs** draw through a cached `E.View('rail', 'Rail', yaw, 8, q, 1)`, where `q = round16(k / 15)`. Copy `Mode7.view(k)` in\\n401\\t  `modules/mode7.js`, and turn the yaw so rigs face the camera's side.\\n402\\t\\n403\\t**Tint pipeline.** Every colour goes through `tint(hex, depth01, emissive)`:\\n404\\t- mix toward the sky's horizon colour by the fog factor `1 - exp(-(d/fogDist)^2 * density)`;\\n405\\t- then apply the time-of-day grade (§4.12) unless the colour is emissive (signal lamps, lamps, lit windows, LED boards, headlight-lit ground);\\n406\\t- cache the results per frame.\\n407\\t\\n408\\t**Night lights.**\\n409\\t- The headlight is an additive trapezoid (`px.blend(g, a, 'add', ...)`) over our track from 3 m to 120 m, plus a boost to rail and sleeper tones\\n410\\t  inside it.\\n411\\t- Lamps and signals get glow discs (`r.glowDisc` or `px.blend` discs).\\n412\\t\\n413\\t**API.**\\n414\\t- `new DH.Rail(compiledRoute, { fov, draw, fog })`\\n415\\t- `rail.draw(r, cam, { actors, tod, weather, t })`\\n416\\t- `rail.project(s, x, y)` returns `[sx, sy, k]` (or null) for the last camera. Used for the HUD brackets, the call glove and mouse picks.\\n417\\t- `rail.pick(sx, sy)` returns the call target or thing under a screen point.\\n418\\t- `rail.visible(s, x, y)` checks that a point isn't hidden behind a crest or tunnel.\\n419\\t\\n420\\t**`#gallery` deep link.** Thirteen fixed shots, each with a caption:\\n421\\t1. straight, day\\n422\\t2. curve plus hill\\n423\\t3. station approach\\n424\\t4. alongside a platform with a crowd\\n425\\t5. close to a signal\\n426\\t6. tunnel mouth\\n427\\t7. truss bridge\\n428\\t8. crossing with gates down\\n429\\t9. quad track with a train alongside\\n430\\t10. night with headlights\\n431\\t11. rain\\n432\\t12. platform rear camera with a train arriving\\n433\\t13. chase camera\\n434\\t\\n435\\t**Tests:**\\n436\\t- Projection math:\\n437\\t  - A point on the centreline straight ahead lands at cx.\\n438\\t  - k halves when the distance doubles.\\n439\\t  - Square pixels: a 1 m × 1 m quad at 20 m measures the same width and height in pixels.\\n440\\t  - Rear view mirrors a point.\\n441\\t- Crest clipping: on a hill, a point beyond the crest returns `visible = false`.\\n442\\t- Strip faces: a platform left of the camera paints its right side face, not the left one.\\n443\\t- `project`, then `pick`, round-trips for a signal.\\n444\\t- The gallery renders all 13 shots headless (`advance(0, { draw: true })` in a browser test) with no errors.\\n445\\t- **Screenshots** for the look review (§8.5).\\n446\\t\\n447\\t### 4.2 Route data and compiler (`20-routes.js`, `21-route-compiler.js`)\\n448\\t\\n449\\tRoutes are **designer-editable scripts**: sorted arrays of `[s, command, params]`. This is how \\\"the remainder of the game could later be built out\\n450\\twith normal designer tools\\\".\\n451\\t\\n452\\t| Command | Params | Produces |\\n453\\t|---|---|---|\\n454\\t| `zone` | id | scenery set, ground colours, backdrop layers from s on |\\n455\\t| `tracks` | `[{ id, x }]` (optional `to` for a diverge over `len`) | track layout from s (e.g. the quad section) |\\n456\\t| `curve` | `{ len, r, dir: 'L'|'R' }` | segment curves |\\n457\\t| `grade` | `{ len, permille }` | heights, eased over 50 m at both ends |\\n458\\t| `limit` | `{ v, until }` (until optional = line speed change) | limit board at s, END board at until, call point |\\n459\\t| `signal` | `{ id, kind: 'start'|'block'|'home'|'shunt', track }` | signal thing, block boundary, ATS beacon at s−600, call point |\\n460\\t| `station` | `{ id, name, en, side, len, stop: { 6: s, 10: s }, roof: [a, b], melody, crowd, landmark, tracks }`; the command's s is the platform's start (its rear end) and `len` its length | platform strips, canopy, boards, props, crowd spawn, approach board at stop−400, a stop marker board for each key of `stop` |\\n461\\t| `tunnel` | `{ len }` | tunnel strips, portal, lamps, sound zone |\\n462\\t| `bridge` | `{ len, kind: 'truss'|'girder', water }` | deck, truss strips, water band, sound zone |\\n463\\t| `crossing` | `{ id, road, cars, peds, special }` | road band, gates, lamps, crossbuck, waiting traffic; `special` adds the obstruction emitter 350 m before |\\n464\\t| `horn` | `{}` | horn board, horn zone of 150 m |\\n465\\t| `workers` | `{ until, limit, lookout }` | slow-order boards (yellow 25 / end), crew things, lookout rig |\\n466\\t| `overpass` | `{ w, h, kind }` | cross strip |\\n467\\t| `viaduct` | `{ len, h }` | the track raised; ground drawn as rooftops far below |\\n468\\t| `landmark` | `{ id, x, side }` | one hand-drawn landmark thing |\\n469\\t| `scenery` | `{ thing, x, ... }` | a manual placement |\\n470\\t\\n471\\t**Auto-generated:**\\n472\\t- catenary masts every 50 m on electrified pairs (lattice in the city, concrete poles in the suburbs);\\n473\\t- km posts every 100 m on the left (\\\"3.4\\\");\\n474\\t- ATS beacons (yellow boxes between the rails);\\n475\\t- call points for every signal, limit board, station approach board, pass board and horn board;\\n476\\t- utility poles with sagging wires along suburban roads; sound walls in the city;\\n477\\t- zone scenery from a **seeded RNG per 50 m cell per side**. Cells occupied by a station, bridge, tunnel, crossing or landmark are skipped.\\n478\\t\\n479\\t**Validation** (run at boot in dev, and as a test):\\n480\\t- stations are in order;\\n481\\t- each stop target for n cars lies within `[platform start + 20·n, platform end]`, so the whole train fits;\\n482\\t- home signals stand before their platform's start;\\n483\\t- every limit ends;\\n484\\t- no signal stands in a portal segment;\\n485\\t- block boundaries are strictly increasing;\\n486\\t- each stage's stops exist on its route.\\n487\\t\\n488\\t**Tests:** compile both routes; the segment count = length / 5; `curve` at mid-curve has the right sign; the height after a 25‰ × 800 m grade is\\n489\\t+20 m (±0.5); a 600 m bridge sets the flag on 120 segments; validation passes; a corrupted script (a stop outside its platform) fails validation.\\n490\\t\\n491\\t### 4.3 Train dynamics and the lever (`30-train.js`)\\n492\\t\\n493\\t**Lever.**\\n494\\t- One axis with positions `EB, B8…B1, N, P1…P5`; the trains in §5.2 decide how many notches.\\n495\\t- `power` moves the lever one position toward P and `brake` one toward B. `neutral` snaps it to N. `eb` jumps to EB.\\n496\\t- Leaving EB needs speed = 0 (`EB RELEASE` prompt), then one `power` press per position.\\n497\\t- Each change: a mechanical clack sfx, the handle animation, and a glove follow (§4.14).\\n498\\t\\n499\\t**Model, per step (dt = 1/120):**\\n500\\t```\\n501\\ttgtP = notch>0 && doorsClosed && !EB ? aP[notch] * min(1, vk[notch] / max(v,1)) * clamp((vmax - v)/5, 0, 1) : 0\\n502\\ttgtB = EB ? aEB : notch<0 ? aB[-notch] : 0\\n503\\tP += (tgtP - P) * (1 - exp(-dt / tauP))\\n504\\tB += (tgtB - B) * (1 - exp(-dt / (tgtB > B ? tauBapply : tauBrelease)))\\n505\\tres = v > 0 ? r0 + r1*v + r2*v*v : 0                    // km/h/s, v in km/h\\n506\\tgrd = -0.0353 * grade_permille(s)                        // km/h/s\\n507\\tadhesion = dry ? 6.0 : TUNING.adhesionRain (2.6)          // km/h/s\\n508\\tif (P > adhesion) slip: P *= .4 for .8 s (SLIP lamp, whine, comfort −2)\\n509\\tif (B > adhesion) slide: B = adhesion * .85 (WSP lamp, judder sound)\\n510\\ta = P - B * sign(v) - res + grd                          // brakes and resistance never push backward\\n511\\tv = v + a*dt (km/h); if a stopped crossing 0 with brakes on → v = 0 (held)\\n512\\trollback: if v == 0 and B < .2 and grd < 0 → may roll backward (ROLLBACK warning; >1 m costs −2 s)\\n513\\ts += v / 3.6 * dt\\n514\\tjerk = (a - aPrev) / dt (smoothed), used by comfort and the smooth-stop judgment\\n515\\t```\\n516\\t\\n517\\t**Default numbers (Halo 8000; every number below is a dev slider or scaled by one):**\\n518\\t\\n519\\t| | P1 | P2 | P3 | P4 | P5 |\\n520\\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n521\\t| a0 (km/h/s) | 0.8 | 1.4 | 2.0 | 2.6 | 3.2 |\\n522\\t| vk (km/h) | 15 | 25 | 35 | 40 | 45 |\\n523\\t\\n524\\t- Brake: Bn = 0.5·n km/h/s (B8 = 4.0). EB 5.0 with τ = 0.3 s.\\n525\\t- τ apply 0.7 s, τ release 0.5 s, τP 0.3 s. vmax 110 km/h.\\n526\\t- Resistance: r0 = 0.03, r1 = 0.0005, r2 = 0.000012.\\n527\\t\\n528\\t**Check values** (these are tests; allow the stated ranges):\\n529\\t- 0→80 km/h on P5, level track: 29–34 s.\\n530\\t- B4 from 80 km/h to a stop: 37–41 s and 425–465 m.\\n531\\t- EB from 80 km/h: 175–195 m.\\n532\\t- Coasting at 80 km/h on level track loses 7–10 km/h per minute.\\n533\\t- (A quick simulation of this model gave 30.4 s, 39.2 s / 439 m, 181 m and 8.2 km/h, and doubling `brakeScale` cut the B4 distance to 232 m.)\\n534\\t- On 25‰ up with P5 at 30 km/h, the train still accelerates. On 25‰ down at N, it gains speed.\\n535\\t\\n536\\t**Tests:** the check values above; the lever passes through every position, clamps at the ends, can't leave EB while moving, and gives no traction with\\n537\\tthe doors open; rain plus P5 from a stand slips (SLIP flag set); dry track never slips; determinism (the same inputs give the same s after 60 s).\\n538\\t\\n539\\t### 4.4 Signals, blocks, other trains, ATS (`31-signals.js`)\\n540\\t\\n541\\t**Blocks.** Each `signal` starts a block that runs to the next signal on that track. A block is occupied while any part of any train actor is in it.\\n542\\t\\n543\\t**Aspects** (Japanese 4-lamp head, top to bottom Y-G-R-Y). The aspect follows from the clear blocks ahead:\\n544\\t- 0 clear → **R**;\\n545\\t- 1 → **Y**;\\n546\\t- 2 → **YG**;\\n547\\t- 3 or more → **G**.\\n548\\t\\n549\\tRoute data can force **YY** (restricted), used at the terminal entry in S5.\\n550\\t\\n551\\t**Aspect speeds:** G = line speed, YG 65, Y 45, YY 25, R = stop before the signal.\\n552\\t\\n553\\t**Starting signals** stay R until the dwell sequence says \\\"ready\\\" (§4.6), and then show the block aspect.\\n554\\t\\n555\\t**Sighting.** A signal's aspect shows on the HUD and becomes callable only once it is *sighted*:\\n556\\t- within 600 m, visible (`rail.visible`) and not in fog;\\n557\\t- in fog, within 180 m.\\n558\\t\\n559\\tLamps draw at least 1 px with a glow, so far signals read as points of light.\\n560\\t\\n561\\t**Train actors** (`DH.TrainActor`):\\n562\\t- Fields: `{ id, track, cars, livery, s, v, dir, lights, script }`.\\n563\\t- The script is a list of legs: `{ vmax, accel, decel, until: s|clock, stopAt, dwell, waitAspect }`.\\n564\\t- Kinds: the preceding freight (S2), the rival express (S4), oncoming locals, parked trains, players' ghosts (§4.23) and the duel opponent.\\n565\\t- Drawing is all strips:\\n566\\t  - body side y 1.0→3.9 m, with windows, doors and a livery stripe pattern, repeated per car (20 m) with gaps;\\n567\\t  - roof, with a pantograph thing on 2 of the cars;\\n568\\t  - skirt and bogies, as dark boxes;\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"c19858c1-c1dc-486e-8c98-335c426b6b98\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:33:18.788Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/work/PLAN.md\",\"content\":\"**Camera.** `{ s, x, h, dir: 1 | -1, fov: 75, horizon: .40, lookX, lookY, pitchPx, rollPx, shake }`.\\n\\n**Projection.** Keep road.js's two passes (near→far to accumulate the bend and clip at crests, then far→near to paint), but use **square pixels**:\\n\\n```\\ndepth = 1 / tan(fov/2)\\nscale = depth / d                    // d = distance ahead of the camera (m), must be > 0.5\\nsx = cx + scale * (wx - camX) * hw   // hw = W/2\\nsy = cy - scale * (wy - camY) * hw   // NOTE: hw again. road.js uses hh here, which squashes the world on wide screens\\ncx = vp.x + W/2 + lookX ;  cy = vp.y + H*horizon + lookY + pitchPx\\n```\\n- `wx = segment bend + lateral offset`; `wy = segment height + object height`.\\n- Pixels per metre at a point: `k = scale * hw`.\\n- Use horizon 0.40 for the cab (the dash covers the bottom 30%) and 0.50 for full-screen exterior cameras.\\n- Draw distance is 360 segments (1.8 km). The fog default fades from 900 m.\\n\\n**Rear view (`dir = -1`).** Walk the segments backward, negate the curve and every lateral offset, and swap the strip ends. This is the forward view\\nof the mirrored line, so the same painter works. It is used by the platform, trackside and Stop-in cameras (§4.13).\\n\\n**Painting, per segment, far→near:**\\n1. **Ground band.**\\n   - The full-width ground row is the zone's ground colour, alternating two tones every 4 segments for a sense of speed.\\n   - On **bridges**, draw water instead: a band 8 m below the deck, with animated glints.\\n   - At **crossings**, draw an asphalt road band across all x, with white edge lines.\\n2. **Track beds.** For each track, a ballast quad 3.0 m wide. Side tracks and sidings are darker and rustier.\\n3. **Sleepers.** Only within 160 m of the camera. Split each segment into 8 slices and draw one dark quad per sleeper (0.24 m along s, 2.4 m\\n   across). Beyond 160 m the ballast tone stands in.\\n4. **Rails.**\\n   - Two quads at ±0.533 m from the track centre, 0.065 m wide, in steel grey.\\n   - Where the rail is 2 px or wider, add a 1-px bright top line.\\n   - When wet, the rails get brighter and pick up the sky colour.\\n5. **Strips** (below): sorted by lateral distance from the camera, farthest first.\\n6. **Things** (below): also sorted farthest lateral first.\\n7. **Train slices**: actors are drawn as strips (§4.4), plus their front-face thing.\\n8. **Catenary wires.**\\n   - For each electrified track, two 1-px lines between this segment's projected points: the contact wire at y +5.1 and the messenger at +5.9.\\n   - The wire zig-zags ±0.2 m, alternating per mast. The converging wires are a strong depth cue; keep them.\\n\\n**Strips** are extrusions along the line: `{ s0, s1, x0, x1, y0, y1, faces: { side, top, under, front, back }, kind }`.\\n- Each face has a drawer `(g, quad, u0, u1, k, tint)`, where u is metres along s, so patterns repeat in world space (windows every 2.5 m, doors\\n  every 5 m, a tactile line).\\n- Which faces show:\\n  - the **side** nearer to camX (x1 if the strip lies left of the camera, x0 if right; none if it spans the camera);\\n  - **top** if the camera is above y1;\\n  - **under** if the camera is below y0 (canopies, overpasses, station roofs);\\n  - **front** or back where s0/s1 falls inside the slice being painted.\\n- Strips are used for:\\n  - platforms: y 0→1.1 m; top face with a white edge line and a yellow tactile strip 0.8 m in;\\n  - canopies (y 3.6→3.9), station buildings, stair housings;\\n  - sound walls, fences, retaining walls;\\n  - truss sides (X pattern);\\n  - tunnel walls and roof (road.js's tunnel, generalised; roof at 6.2 m; portal face; a wall lamp every 25 m);\\n  - overpasses: cross strips spanning all x, with an under face;\\n  - rows of houses and warehouses, viaduct parapets;\\n  - **trains** (§4.4).\\n\\n**Things** are point objects: `{ s, x, y, w, h, draw(g, sx, sy, k, ctx) }`.\\n- `(sx, sy)` is the screen position of the base centre; `k` is pixels per metre.\\n- Every drawer picks a level of detail from k: below 0.4, a dot or nothing; below 3, a 2–3 tone silhouette; 3 and up, full detail with\\n  `E.tones` shading.\\n- `E.sprite` sprites and `E.shapeSprite` bakes are wrapped as things with metres-per-pixel.\\n- **Humanoids and Blobs** draw through a cached `E.View('rail', 'Rail', yaw, 8, q, 1)`, where `q = round16(k / 15)`. Copy `Mode7.view(k)` in\\n  `modules/mode7.js`, and turn the yaw so rigs face the camera's side.\\n\\n**Tint pipeline.** Every colour goes through `tint(hex, depth01, emissive)`:\\n- mix toward the sky's horizon colour by the fog factor `1 - exp(-(d/fogDist)^2 * density)`;\\n- then apply the time-of-day grade (§4.12) unless the colour is emissive (signal lamps, lamps, lit windows, LED boards, headlight-lit ground);\\n- cache the results per frame.\\n\\n**Night lights.**\\n- The headlight is an additive trapezoid (`px.blend(g, a, 'add', ...)`) over our track from 3 m to 120 m, plus a boost to rail and sleeper tones\\n  inside it.\\n- Lamps and signals get glow discs (`r.glowDisc` or `px.blend` discs).\\n\\n**API.**\\n- `new DH.Rail(compiledRoute, { fov, draw, fog })`\\n- `rail.draw(r, cam, { actors, tod, weather, t })`\\n- `rail.project(s, x, y)` returns `[sx, sy, k]` (or null) for the last camera. Used for the HUD brackets, the call glove and mouse picks.\\n- `rail.pick(sx, sy)` returns the call target or thing under a screen point.\\n- `rail.visible(s, x, y)` checks that a point isn't hidden behind a crest or tunnel.\\n\\n**`#gallery` deep link.** Thirteen fixed shots, each with a caption:\\n1. straight, day\\n2. curve plus hill\\n3. station approach\\n4. alongside a platform with a crowd\\n5. close to a signal\\n6. tunnel mouth\\n7. truss bridge\\n8. crossing with gates down\\n9. quad track with a train alongside\\n10. night with headlights\\n11. rain\\n12. platform rear camera with a train arriving\\n13. chase camera\\n\\n**Tests:**\\n- Projection math:\\n  - A point on the centreline straight ahead lands at cx.\\n  - k halves when the distance doubles.\\n  - Square pixels: a 1 m × 1 m quad at 20 m measures the same width and height in pixels.\\n  - Rear view mirrors a point.\\n- Crest clipping: on a hill, a point beyond the crest returns `visible = false`.\\n- Strip faces: a platform left of the camera paints its right side face, not the left one.\\n- `project`, then `pick`, round-trips for a signal.\\n- The gallery renders all 13 shots headless (`advance(0, { draw: true })` in a browser test) with no errors.\\n- **Screenshots** for the look review (§8.5).\\n\\n### 4.2 Route data and compiler (`20-routes.js`, `21-route-compiler.js`)\\n\\nRoutes are **designer-editable scripts**: sorted arrays of `[s, command, params]`. This is how \\\"the remainder of the game could later be built out\\nwith normal designer tools\\\".\\n\\n| Command | Params | Produces |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `zone` | id | scenery set, ground colours, backdrop layers from s on |\\n| `tracks` | `[{ id, x }]` (optional `to` for a diverge over `len`) | track layout from s (e.g. the quad section) |\\n| `curve` | `{ len, r, dir: 'L'|'R' }` | segment curves |\\n| `grade` | `{ len, permille }` | heights, eased over 50 m at both ends |\\n| `limit` | `{ v, until }` (until optional = line speed change) | limit board at s, END board at until, call point |\\n| `signal` | `{ id, kind: 'start'|'block'|'home'|'shunt', track }` | signal thing, block boundary, ATS beacon at s−600, call point |\\n| `station` | `{ id, name, en, side, len, stop: { 6: s, 10: s }, roof: [a, b], melody, crowd, landmark, tracks }`; the command's s is the platform's start (its rear end) and `len` its length | platform strips, canopy, boards, props, crowd spawn, approach board at stop−400, a stop marker board for each key of `stop` |\\n| `tunnel` | `{ len }` | tunnel strips, portal, lamps, sound zone |\\n| `bridge` | `{ len, kind: 'truss'|'girder', water }` | deck, truss strips, water band, sound zone |\\n| `crossing` | `{ id, road, cars, peds, special }` | road band, gates, lamps, crossbuck, waiting traffic; `special` adds the obstruction emitter 350 m before |\\n| `horn` | `{}` | horn board, horn zone of 150 m |\\n| `workers` | `{ until, limit, lookout }` | slow-order boards (yellow 25 / end), crew things, lookout rig |\\n| `overpass` | `{ w, h, kind }` | cross strip |\\n| `viaduct` | `{ len, h }` | the track raised; ground drawn as rooftops far below |\\n| `landmark` | `{ id, x, side }` | one hand-drawn landmark thing |\\n| `scenery` | `{ thing, x, ... }` | a manual placement |\\n\\n**Auto-generated:**\\n- catenary masts every 50 m on electrified pairs (lattice in the city, concrete poles in the suburbs);\\n- km posts every 100 m on the left (\\\"3.4\\\");\\n- ATS beacons (yellow boxes between the rails);\\n- call points for every signal, limit board, station approach board, pass board and horn board;\\n- utility poles with sagging wires along suburban roads; sound walls in the city;\\n- zone scenery from a **seeded RNG per 50 m cell per side**. Cells occupied by a station, bridge, tunnel, crossing or landmark are skipped.\\n\\n**Validation** (run at boot in dev, and as a test):\\n- stations are in order;\\n- each stop target for n cars lies within `[platform start + 20·n, platform end]`, so the whole train fits;\\n- home signals stand before their platform's start;\\n- every limit ends;\\n- no signal stands in a portal segment;\\n- block boundaries are strictly increasing;\\n- each stage's stops exist on its route.\\n\\n**Tests:** compile both routes; the segment count = length / 5; `curve` at mid-curve has the right sign; the height after a 25‰ × 800 m grade is\\n+20 m (±0.5); a 600 m bridge sets the flag on 120 segments; validation passes; a corrupted script (a stop outside its platform) fails validation.\\n\\n### 4.3 Train dynamics and the lever (`30-train.js`)\\n\\n**Lever.**\\n- One axis with positions `EB, B8…B1, N, P1…P5`; the trains in §5.2 decide how many notches.\\n- `power` moves the lever one position toward P and `brake` one toward B. `neutral` snaps it to N. `eb` jumps to EB.\\n- Leaving EB needs speed = 0 (`EB RELEASE` prompt), then one `power` press per position.\\n- Each change: a mechanical clack sfx, the handle animation, and a glove follow (§4.14).\\n\\n**Model, per step (dt = 1/120):**\\n```\\ntgtP = notch>0 && doorsClosed && !EB ? aP[notch] * min(1, vk[notch] / max(v,1)) * clamp((vmax - v)/5, 0, 1) : 0\\ntgtB = EB ? aEB : notch<0 ? aB[-notch] : 0\\nP += (tgtP - P) * (1 - exp(-dt / tauP))\\nB += (tgtB - B) * (1 - exp(-dt / (tgtB > B ? tauBapply : tauBrelease)))\\nres = v > 0 ? r0 + r1*v + r2*v*v : 0                    // km/h/s, v in km/h\\ngrd = -0.0353 * grade_permille(s)                        // km/h/s\\nadhesion = dry ? 6.0 : TUNING.adhesionRain (2.6)          // km/h/s\\nif (P > adhesion) slip: P *= .4 for .8 s (SLIP lamp, whine, comfort −2)\\nif (B > adhesion) slide: B = adhesion * .85 (WSP lamp, judder sound)\\na = P - B * sign(v) - res + grd                          // brakes and resistance never push backward\\nv = v + a*dt (km/h); if a stopped crossing 0 with brakes on → v = 0 (held)\\nrollback: if v == 0 and B < .2 and grd < 0 → may roll backward (ROLLBACK warning; >1 m costs −2 s)\\ns += v / 3.6 * dt\\njerk = (a - aPrev) / dt (smoothed), used by comfort and the smooth-stop judgment\\n```\\n\\n**Default numbers (Halo 8000; every number below is a dev slider or scaled by one):**\\n\\n| | P1 | P2 | P3 | P4 | P5 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| a0 (km/h/s) | 0.8 | 1.4 | 2.0 | 2.6 | 3.2 |\\n| vk (km/h) | 15 | 25 | 35 | 40 | 45 |\\n\\n- Brake: Bn = 0.5·n km/h/s (B8 = 4.0). EB 5.0 with τ = 0.3 s.\\n- τ apply 0.7 s, τ release 0.5 s, τP 0.3 s. vmax 110 km/h.\\n- Resistance: r0 = 0.03, r1 = 0.0005, r2 = 0.000012.\\n\\n**Check values** (these are tests; allow the stated ranges):\\n- 0→80 km/h on P5, level track: 29–34 s.\\n- B4 from 80 km/h to a stop: 37–41 s and 425–465 m.\\n- EB from 80 km/h: 175–195 m.\\n- Coasting at 80 km/h on level track loses 7–10 km/h per minute.\\n- (A quick simulation of this model gave 30.4 s, 39.2 s / 439 m, 181 m and 8.2 km/h, and doubling `brakeScale` cut the B4 distance to 232 m.)\\n- On 25‰ up with P5 at 30 km/h, the train still accelerates. On 25‰ down at N, it gains speed.\\n\\n**Tests:** the check values above; the lever passes through every position, clamps at the ends, can't leave EB while moving, and gives no traction with\\nthe doors open; rain plus P5 from a stand slips (SLIP flag set); dry track never slips; determinism (the same inputs give the same s after 60 s).\\n\\n### 4.4 Signals, blocks, other trains, ATS (`31-signals.js`)\\n\\n**Blocks.** Each `signal` starts a block that runs to the next signal on that track. A block is occupied while any part of any train actor is in it.\\n\\n**Aspects** (Japanese 4-lamp head, top to bottom Y-G-R-Y). The aspect follows from the clear blocks ahead:\\n- 0 clear → **R**;\\n- 1 → **Y**;\\n- 2 → **YG**;\\n- 3 or more → **G**.\\n\\nRoute data can force **YY** (restricted), used at the terminal entry in S5.\\n\\n**Aspect speeds:** G = line speed, YG 65, Y 45, YY 25, R = stop before the signal.\\n\\n**Starting signals** stay R until the dwell sequence says \\\"ready\\\" (§4.6), and then show the block aspect.\\n\\n**Sighting.** A signal's aspect shows on the HUD and becomes callable only once it is *sighted*:\\n- within 600 m, visible (`rail.visible`) and not in fog;\\n- in fog, within 180 m.\\n\\nLamps draw at least 1 px with a glow, so far signals read as points of light.\\n\\n**Train actors** (`DH.TrainActor`):\\n- Fields: `{ id, track, cars, livery, s, v, dir, lights, script }`.\\n- The script is a list of legs: `{ vmax, accel, decel, until: s|clock, stopAt, dwell, waitAspect }`.\\n- Kinds: the preceding freight (S2), the rival express (S4), oncoming locals, parked trains, players' ghosts (§4.23) and the duel opponent.\\n- Drawing is all strips:\\n  - body side y 1.0→3.9 m, with windows, doors and a livery stripe pattern, repeated per car (20 m) with gaps;\\n  - roof, with a pantograph thing on 2 of the cars;\\n  - skirt and bogies, as dark boxes;\",\"numLines\":230,\"startLine\":339,\"totalLines\":1944}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_019HbdVWVqfcrMmcpoW8ydKr\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"fe270b15-0160-410f-bfbe-5f75c4fe9f9d\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":200,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"4a25d59e-4367-4779-ae31-864e54689120\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":347,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":147,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"20c0243b-232d-4bc5-9462-e3e59c011b28\"}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2VyojpQhLbuuN5Gffd\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":7516,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":423199,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":7516},\"output_tokens\":3,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"df968c89-67a8-4be4-af09-fc87ad73137f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:33:23.725Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2VyVdgfE4N8QpuPcSo\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":2799,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1968,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1968,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3500,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"a11aad98-202b-42db-8d5d-63c62ee65a65\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3600,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"fbccca23-7add-4cb7-a0e6-93dfdc345045\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3700,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"805f195a-0929-442c-bb17-bd904da6b21b\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3850,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"111f9b40-0f29-4353-bdc4-28ee48112057\"}"]}]}],"moreFiles":5,"changedFiles":["/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174743__x8534bd/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174755__x531263/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174834__x4394d1/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183009__xe162c9/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183153__xf5fb84/work/TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153/.gitignore","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183153__xf5fb84/work/TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153/package-lock.json","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183303__xf5fb84/agent.out"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HR6DFJiMkBKFVNatzPTgFk","permission_d