rough of every stage (the fair Autopilot playing each stage headless through game.advance) was the most helpful: it exercised every wave, cue, boss phase and the scene flow in seconds.',\n    'The least helpful was a broad screenshot sweep of menus at several window sizes: it confirmed what the layout code already guaranteed and found nothing new.'\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    'The fixed-step headless loop (game.advance) and the Humanoid rig: rigs update deterministically in Node, so hit volumes from real joints could be tested without a browser, and the same rigs draw beautifully from any bearing.',\n    'Yes: the handoff has no 3D engine, so a light-gun shooter needed our own perspective renderer; we also guessed the box-face winding wrong at first and had to verify it numerically. The View class cannot look up, so billboards clamp their pitch.',\n    'Rail3D, a z-buffered perspective software rasterizer that samples the engine\\'s material tiles in its five light bands and composites engine rigs as depth-tested billboards.',\n    'Fitting a first-person 3D game into a 2D engine built around ground views: the camera, culling, fog and lighting had to be rebuilt beside it, while the menus, input, audio and boot came for free.',\n    'Four patches, logged in engine/PATCHES.md: long guns (rifle, SMG, shotgun, launcher, sniper rifle, minigun, harpoon, flare pistol) and a cutlass on Humanoid with a fore-grip hand; PlayStation button glyphs by default with an Xbox option; mouse-wheel codes that can be bound; and access to the sfx and music buses for our voice synth.',\n    'A small perspective camera in the 2D engine: E.shapeMesh and E.materialTile already exist for a 3D renderer, so a reference rasterizer using them would make light-gun, FPS and rail games first-class.'\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    'It names the whole job, from the controller-first 10-foot UI to the attract mode, dev sliders, online play and the exit interview, so nothing had to be guessed about what done means.'