{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghBRaDqsQAEKiioPujmc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0135TDijq4MWBCuQMPtE3UMe","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/99-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index(\"const FEEDBACK = {\"); b=s.index(\"const HUBINFO\")\nnew='''const FEEDBACK = {\n  testing: [\n    'About 40. The bot playthroughs and boss runs found 23 game bugs, the content validator found 11 cover points whose peeks could not be seen or shot from the players\\\\' slots, and screenshots found 6 visual problems (missing font glyphs for the PlayStation buttons, a glass wall and a turbine-hall wall blocking the lift view, an over-lit market floor, a windscreen in the boat view, cutscene actors facing away).',\n    '10 were game breaking: areas or bosses that could never be finished (the Bulldog\\\\'s fuel tank inside its collider, canal enemies left behind the boat, Barracuda boarding outside the field of view, a shield trooper with no state for hiding, funicular terraces hidden by the cliff, a lighthouse collider hiding an enemy, rappellers stuck on the lift\\\\'s ropes, Hammerhead\\\\'s rig turned to NaN by a missing velocity, the Seawolf hovering out of view), and Training could end in a game over.',\n    'The bot playthroughs: the Autopilot playing every stage headless through game.advance (expert in god mode for completion, fair for balance, showoff for the whole ARCADE with its cutscenes). They found every unfinishable area in seconds and gave the clear times the area timers are set from.',\n    'The 3D math and rasterizer unit tests: they guard projection, rays and depth, but never failed once the real code ran; every renderer problem was found by looking at screenshots instead.'\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    'The deterministic fixed-step loop with headless game.advance, and the Humanoid rigs: they update the same in Node and in the browser, so hit volumes from real joints, the bots and the AI Hub replicas all agree, and the same rigs draw from any bearing as billboards.',\n    'Yes. The handoff has no 3D engine, so a first-person light-gun game needed its own renderer. E.View cannot look up (pitch is clamped), so billboards fake the angle. A rig update with vx but no vy put NaN into the rig\\\\'s walk phase for good, which made a boss unhittable until a test found it. The Humanoid constructor draws from Math.random, so the game reseeds each rig\\\\'s clock for determinism.',\n    'Rail3D: a z-buffered, perspective-correct software rasterizer that samples E.materialTile textures in the engine\\\\'s five light bands with Bayer dithering and fog, draws E.shapeMesh objects, and composites engine rigs as depth-tested billboards drawn by the engine from the bearing they are seen at.',\n    'Fitting a first-person 3D shooter into a 2D engine built around ground views: the camera, culling, lighting, fog, rays and colliders had to be built beside it, while the menus, settings, rebinding, input, audio, boot screen and tables came for free and worked first time.',\n    'Four patches, logged in engine/PATCHES.md: long guns (rifle, SMG, shotgun, launcher, sniper rifle, minigun, harpoon, flare pistol) and a cutlass on Humanoid with a fore-grip hand; PlayStation button glyphs by default with an Xbox option; mouse-wheel codes that can be bound; access to the sfx and music buses for the voice synth.',\n    'A small perspective camera module beside Mode 7: E.shapeMesh and E.materialTile already exist for a 3D renderer, so a reference rasterizer using them would make light-gun, rail and FPS games first-class in the 2D package (and rig.update could reject NaN input).'\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    'It defines done completely: the controller-first 10-foot UI, prompts that follow the device, the attract mode, live dev sliders, couch and online play, the About screen and the exit interview, so the scope never had to be guessed.',\n    'The plan was written for another run, so its file names and timestamps differ from TASK.md\\\\'s (TASK.md wins), and the handoff it names has no 3D engine for a source that is first-person 3D.',\n    'The volume: three stages, eight bosses and mini-bosses, a software renderer, split screen and AI Hub co-op, all art and sound in code, and then balancing it with bots so every area can be finished in its time.',\n    'Say whether the repository\\\\'s 3D engine may be used, and give a time or token budget so scope can be planned against it; ask for the feedback numbers to come from a log the tests keep.'\n  ]\n};\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\ns=s.replace(\"  testing: 'Headless Node tests through game.advance and game.debug behind one command (node tests/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count, including bot playthroughs of every stage; Playwright screenshots for what scripts cannot judge; the AI Hub harness in headless Chromium',\",\"  testing: 'Allowed: headless tools as much as liked (game.advance and game.debug), screenshots only for what a script cannot check, one command that prints only failures and a count, no subagents or workflows to review. Used: node tests/run.mjs (535 checks in 19 suites, plus --playthrough bot runs of every stage and the whole ARCADE, --browser at four window sizes, --hub for the four AI Hub harness scenarios in headless Chromium)',\")\ns=s.replace(\"  usage: 'unknown'\",\"  usage: 'Session time about 2 hours 15 minutes (18:31 to about 20:45); API cost and token counts unknown'\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\nnode tools/build.mjs 2>&1 | tail -1 && python3 -c \"import json;d=json.load(open('../exit-interview.json'));print(len(d['testingFeedback']),len(d['engineFeedback']),len(d['promptFeedback']),list(d['aiHub'].keys()))\"","description":"Write final feedback and rebuild; verify exit interview parses"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1266,"cache_read_input_tokens":888384,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1266},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcbe5b02-218b-4276-a1cd-eeebf017e39a","uuid":"ec4c5b1f-e351-47a3-be76-24475302af0d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:31:00.043Z","request_id":"req_011CghBRZpYMqBrCFDUYsph9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0135TDijq4MWBCuQMPtE3UMe":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183153__xf5fb84/work/TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153 && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/99-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index(\"const FEEDBACK = {\"); b=s.index(\"const HUBINFO\")\nnew='''const FEEDBACK = {\n  testing: [\n    'About 40. The bot playthroughs and boss runs found 23 game bugs, the content validator found 11 cover points whose peeks could not be seen or shot from the players\\\\' slots, and screenshots found 6 visual problems (missing font glyphs for the PlayStation buttons, a glass wall and a turbine-hall wall blocking the lift view, an over-lit market floor, a windscreen in the boat view, cutscene actors facing away).',\n    '10 were game breaking: areas or bosses that could never be finished (the Bulldog\\\\'s fuel tank inside its collider, canal enemies left behind the boat, Barracuda boarding outside the field of view, a shield trooper with no state for hiding, funicular terraces hidden by the cliff, a lighthouse collider hiding an enemy, rappellers stuck on the lift\\\\'s ropes, Hammerhead\\\\'s rig turned to NaN by a missing velocity, the Seawolf hovering out of view), and Training could end in a game over.',\n    'The bot playthroughs: the Autopilot playing every stage headless through game.advance (expert in god mode for completion, fair for balance, showoff for the whole ARCADE with its cutscenes). They found every unfinishable area in seconds and gave the clear times the area timers are set from.',\n    'The 3D math and rasterizer unit tests: they guard projection, rays and depth, but never failed once the real code ran; every renderer problem was found by looking at screenshots instead.'\n  ],\n  engine: [\n    'The deterministic fixed-step loop with headless game.advance, and the Humanoid rigs: they update the same in Node and in the browser, so hit volumes from real joints, the bots and the AI Hub replicas all agree, and the same rigs draw from any bearing as billboards.',\n    'Yes. The handoff has no 3D engine, so a first-person light-gun game needed its own renderer. E.View cannot look up (pitch is clamped), so billboards fake the angle. A rig update with vx but no vy put NaN into the rig\\\\'s walk phase for good, which made a boss unhittable until a test found it. The Humanoid constructor draws from Math.random, so the game reseeds each rig\\\\'s clock for determinism.',\n    'Rail3D: a z-buffered, perspective-correct software rasterizer that samples E.materialTile textures in the engine\\\\'s five light bands with Bayer dithering and fog, draws E.shapeMesh objects, and composites engine rigs as depth-tested billboards drawn by the engine from the bearing they are seen at.',\n    'Fitting a first-person 3D shooter into a 2D engine built around ground views: the camera, culling, lighting, fog, rays and colliders had to be built beside it, while the menus, settings, rebinding, input, audio, boot screen and tables came for free and worked first time.',\n    'Four patches, logged in engine/PATCHES.md: long guns (rifle, SMG, shotgun, launcher, sniper rifle, minigun, harpoon, flare pistol) and a cutlass on Humanoid with a fore-grip hand; PlayStation button glyphs by default with an Xbox option; mouse-wheel codes that can be bound; access to the sfx and music buses for the voice synth.',\n    'A small perspective camera module beside Mode 7: E.shapeMesh and E.materialTile already exist for a 3D renderer, so a reference rasterizer using them would make light-gun, rail and FPS games first-class in the 2D package (and rig.update could reject NaN input).'\n  ],\n  prompt: [\n    'It defines done completely: the controller-first 10-foot UI, prompts that follow the device, the attract mode, live dev sliders, couch and online play, the About screen and the exit interview, so the scope never had to be guessed.',\n    'The plan was written for another run, so its file names and timestamps differ from TASK.md\\\\'s (TASK.md wins), and the handoff it names has no 3D engine for a source that is first-person 3D.',\n    'The volume: three stages, eight bosses and mini-bosses, a software renderer, split screen and AI Hub co-op, all art and sound in code, and then balancing it with bots so every area can be finished in its time.',\n    'Say whether the repository\\\\'s 3D engine may be used, and give a time or token budget so scope can be planned against it; ask for the feedback numbers to come from a log the tests keep.'\n  ]\n};\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\ns=s.replace(\"  testing: 'Headless Node tests through game.advance and game.debug behind one command (node tests/run.mjs) that prints only failures and a count, including bot playthroughs of every stage; Playwright screenshots for what scripts cannot judge; the AI Hub harness in headless Chromium',\",\"  testing: 'Allowed: headless tools as much as liked (game.advance and game.debug), screenshots only for what a script cannot check, one command that prints only failures and a count, no subagents or workflows to review. Used: node tests/run.mjs (535 checks in 19 suites, plus --playthrough bot runs of every stage and the whole ARCADE, --browser at four window sizes, --hub for the four AI Hub harness scenarios in headless Chromium)',\")\ns=s.replace(\"  usage: 'unknown'\",\"  usage: 'Session time about 2 hours 15 minutes (18:31 to about 20:45); API cost and token counts unknown'\")\nopen(p