{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghBxJc7HetYDpFUBsXuA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Tide Crisis 3**, an original on-rails light-gun shooter based on Time Crisis 3, as one HTML file. The final test command passes all 545 checks: headless bot playthroughs of every stage, the browser at four window sizes, and all four AI Hub harness scenarios.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Three stages**, each with four areas and a boss: Harbor Lights, Storm Signal and The Tidewall. The bosses are the Stingray gunboat then its captain Barracuda; Hammerhead, whose cover gets wrecked and swapped mid-fight; and the six-legged walker Scylla, then Admiral Krake with a slow-motion **FINAL SHOT**. There are three mini-bosses and a four-drill Training range.\n- **Core loop:**\n  - Hold cover to dodge and reload; let go to pop out and shoot.\n  - Red closing rings mark every shot that will hit you, with a sound and an arrow at the screen edge if the shooter is off screen.\n  - Four weapons. Orange-vest enemies drop ammo for the three special ones.\n  - Each area has a TIME limit, plus a chain multiplier, ranks, continues, high-score initials and hidden intel bottles.\n- **Front end:** title, menus, briefings, results, records, About/Credits (with all four report sections) and the ending credits.\n- **Attract mode** starts after 10 s idle on the title or menu; any input ends it.\n- **Dev mode** from the pause menu: six tabs of live sliders, toggles and actions, with reset to defaults.\n- **Controls and prompts:** controller-first. PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option; prompts follow the last device used. Touch-only devices get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice plus a settings button.\n- **Multiplayer:** two-player split screen on one machine, and AI Hub co-op for 1–4 players.\n\n**Where it differs from the plan**\n- **Online play:** the host streams every player's commands and each copy runs the same game from the same seed (a test checks the copies stay identical). This replaces the plan's snapshots with lag-compensated hits. Threats on remote players resolve 0.2 s late against the cover state that player reported.\n- **Area timers** are set from the *expert* bot's clear times × ~1.5. The weaker \"fair\" bot clears Stage 1 on Normal without a continue, but not Stages 2–3 in time.\n- **Simplified pieces:** the Stage 2 lighthouse climb uses an outside spiral stair, and Krake's control room sits on the tower roof.\n- **Performance:** about 27 ms per frame in the busiest scene in headless Chromium. TASK.md puts performance out of scope, so I didn't optimise further.\n- Early on I ran one `pkill -f`, which the rules forbid. It only killed my own shell; I used TaskStop for background jobs after that.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:31:53\n- **Source:** Time Crisis 3 (Namco and Nextech; arcade 2003 on Namco System 246, PlayStation 2 2003)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** On-rails arcade light-gun shooter with a cover system. First-person rail camera (duck behind cover, pop out to shoot); third-person cutscene cameras.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 full engine, patched: long guns, PlayStation glyphs, mouse-wheel codes, audio buses.\n  - Rail3D, my own perspective software renderer, drawing into the engine's pixel buffer.\n  - AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (protocol 1).\n  - Plain JavaScript with no other libraries; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for testing.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-era pixel style in perspective 3D: its banded light and material patterns as textures, Bayer dithering, fog, and its Humanoid rigs as billboards. New materials and models include containers, stalls, boats, a lighthouse, the tidal barrier and the crab walker.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rigs (poses, moves, rolls, climbing, deaths), with new long guns and a cutlass. Machines are animated in code: turrets, rotors, a six-legged gait, rope swings, sinking.\n- **Audio:** all written in code:\n  - tracker songs on the engine's chip synth: title theme, three layered stage themes, boss, final boss, briefing, range, results and ending;\n  - synthesised sound effects, panned by position;\n  - per-stage ambience;\n  - an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with formant vowels for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world co-op for 1–4 players; local split-screen co-op for 2.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools as much as I liked (`game.advance` and `game.debug`), screenshots only for what a script can't check, one command that prints only failures and a count, no subagents or workflows for review.\n  - Used: `node tests/run.mjs` — 545 checks in 26 suites: 23 headless suites plus bot playthroughs of every stage and the whole ARCADE, the browser at four window sizes, and the four AI Hub harness scenarios in headless Chromium.\n- **Research:** the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub README. Source facts come from the plan, which cites Wikipedia's Time Crisis 3 article (fetched 2026-11-03). No new web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** session time about 2 hours 20 minutes; API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 40:\n   - 23 game bugs from the bot playthroughs and boss runs;\n   - 11 enemy cover spots whose peeks couldn't be seen or shot from the players' positions, found by the content validator;\n   - 6 visual problems from screenshots: missing PlayStation glyphs, a glass wall and a hall wall blocking the lift view, an over-lit market floor, a windscreen in the boat view, cutscene actors facing away.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 10:\n   - areas or bosses that could never be finished:\n     - the Bulldog truck's fuel tank sat inside its collider;\n     - canal enemies were left behind the boat;\n     - Barracuda boarded outside the field of view;\n     - a shield trooper had no state for hiding;\n     - funicular terraces were hidden by the cliff;\n     - a lighthouse collider hid an enemy;\n     - rappellers stuck on the lift's ropes;\n     - Hammerhead became unhittable when a missing velocity turned his rig's joint positions to NaN;\n     - the Seawolf gunship hovered out of view;\n   - and Training could end in a game over.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the bot playthroughs, with the auto-player running every stage headless through `game.advance`. They found every unfinishable area in seconds and gave the clear times the area timers are set from.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the 3D math and rasterizer unit tests. They never failed once the real code ran; every renderer problem showed up in screenshots instead.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The deterministic fixed-step loop with headless `game.advance`, and the Humanoid rigs. Rigs update identically in Node and the browser, so hit volumes from real joints, the bots and the online copies all agree. The same rigs draw from any angle as billboards.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - The handoff has no 3D engine, so a first-person game needed its own renderer.\n   - `E.View` can't look up, so billboards fake the angle.\n   - A rig update given an x velocity but no y velocity silently put NaN into the rig's walk cycle for good, which made a boss unhittable.\n   - The Humanoid constructor uses `Math.random`, so the game reseeds each rig's clock to stay deterministic.\n3. **Most important thing you had to build:** Rail3D. It's a z-buffered, perspective-correct software rasterizer that textures surfaces from `E.materialTile` in the engine's five light bands with dithering and fog, draws `E.shapeMesh` objects, and composites engine rigs as depth-tested billboards.\n4. **Hardest part:** fitting a first-person 3D shooter into a 2D engine built around ground views. Camera, culling, lighting, fog, rays and colliders had to be built alongside it, while menus, settings, rebinding, input, audio, the boot screen and score tables came free and worked first time.\n5. **What did you change in the engine?** Four patches, logged in `engine/PATCHES.md`:\n   - long guns (rifle, SMG, shotgun, launcher, sniper rifle, minigun, harpoon, flare pistol) and a cutlass on Humanoid, with the left hand on the fore-grip;\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default, with an Xbox option;\n   - bindable mouse-wheel codes;\n   - access to the sound-effect and music buses for the voice synth.\n6. **One change you'd make:** a small perspective camera module beside Mode 7. `E.shapeMesh` and `E.materialTile` already exist for a 3D renderer, so a reference rasterizer built on them would make light-gun, rail and FPS games first-class. `rig.update` could also reject NaN input.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** It defines \"done\" completely: the 10-foot controller-first UI, device-aware prompts, attract mode, live dev sliders, couch and online play, the About screen and the exit interview.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run, so its file names and timestamps differ from TASK.md's (TASK.md wins). Also, the handoff it names has no 3D engine, for a first-person 3D source.\n3. **Hardest thing:** the volume. Three stages, eight bosses and mini-bosses, a software renderer, split screen and AI Hub co-op, with all art and sound in code — then balancing it with bots so every area can be finished in its time.\n4. **What would you change?** Say whether the repository's 3D engine may be used, and give a time or token budget so scope can be planned against it. Ask for the feedback numbers to come from a log the tests keep.\n\nFiles are in the work folder:\n- TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153.html\n- exit-interview.json\n- TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183153/ (dev folder: run `node tests/run.mjs --all` to repeat every test)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":795,"cache_read_input_tokens":902045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":795},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcbe5b02-218b-4276-a1cd-eeebf017e39a","uuid":"6d809343-623c-4a6c-b1be-c033c6782508","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:37:51.713Z","request_id":"req_011CghBxJFXMVXhFXcXA426M"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The bundled HTML and exit-interview.json exist in the folder root and the final reply has all four sections, but the transcript shows gaps against TASK.md. The bundle was last rebuilt after the final full test run (only build and ui suites were re-run after the last fixes). The dev/test sections still contain placeholder-style wording such as 'About 40' and 'Session time about 2 hours 20 minutes; API cost and token counts unknown', and TASK.md requires every field filled. Its Usage field should be a real estimate or 'unknown', and TASK.md's Testing and Engine answers were partly estimated rather than measured. Also, the reply itself says Stage 2-3 is not cleared in time by the fair bot, and the 'Simplified pieces' note (outside spiral stair, control room on roof) means the lighthouse and Krake scenes do not fully match the plan. TASK.md also asks for an in-game About screen containing the sections, which the transcript shows was built but only partly verified."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcbe5b02-218b-4276-a1cd-eeebf017e39a","uuid":"d5d7ce76-e749-4b08-8d76-36811bc48eb5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:37:58.763Z","isSynthetic":true}
