{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Am8fHJ7RVL4M6YQ1ranvZq","type":"tool_result","content":"rect [ 109.5, 181, 208, 19 ] css [ 640, 571.5, 427, 240, 1280, 720 ] {\n  ptr: [ 213.66666666666666, 190.5 ],\n  last: 'keyboard',\n  i: 0,\n  active: true\n}\n[ 1, 'CO-OP' ]\nafter esc {\n  scene: 'play',\n  paused: false,\n  menuOpen: undefined,\n  focus: '\\n' +\n    '\\n' +\n    '\\n' +\n    '\\n' +\n    '/*!\\n' +\n    ' * my-3D2dge v0.85.2  (My \"3D\" 2D Game Engine)\\n' +\n    ' * Procedural pixel-puppet engine for every 2D perspective.\\n' +\n    ' * https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge  (MIT License)\\n' +\n    ' *\\n' +\n    ' * READ THIS FIRST (humans and AI models extending a my-3D2dge game)\\n' +\n    ' * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n' +\n    ' * 0. EVERY GAME STARTS WITH  const E = My3D2dge;  and then uses E.Game, E.px, ...\\n' +\n    ' * 1. WORLD SPACE IS 3D. x = east, y = south, z = up. One floor tile = 16 units.\\n' +\n    ' *    Game logic never thinks about screen pixels.\\n' +\n    ' * 2. A VIEW turns world points into screen pixels. Swap the view and the whole game\\n' +\n    ' *    re-renders from another angle: E.VIEWS.iso, .threequarter, .topdown,\\n' +\n    ' *    .brawler, .side  (or build your own: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)).\\n' +\n    ' * 3. CHARACTERS ARE RIGS, NOT SPRITES. E.Humanoid and E.Blob recompute their\\n' +\n    ' *    joints from math every step, in 3D, then project them. That is why they move\\n' +\n    ' *    fluidly and can face any angle in any view without extra art. Small things\\n' +\n    ' *    (coins, bullets, icons) can be pixel sprites written as strings: E.sprite().\\n' +\n    ' * 4. PIXEL RULES. Draw with E.px (rect, line, disc, ell, poly, sprite...). Every shape\\n' +\n    ' *    snaps to whole pixels. Never use ctx.arc / ctx.stroke / ctx.fillText on the game\\n' +\n    ' *    buffer: they anti-alias and break the look. Translucency and glow go through\\n' +\n    ' *    px.blend / polyDither / ddisc / r.glowDisc: stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1\\n' +\n    \" *    hardware (E.style.trans = 'dither' switches to NES / Genesis style dithering).\\n\" +\n    ' * 5. FRAME ORDER (handled by the Renderer):\\n' +\n    ' *      background (r.sky, map.drawFloor or level.draw) -> decals (ground marks, shadows)\\n' +\n    ' *      -> sorted queue (walls, props, actors, particles) -> lighting -> overlays -> present\\n' +\n    ' *    Anything that can be in front of or behind something else goes through\\n' +\n    ' *    r.queue(x, y, z, fn), r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, opts) or r.sprite(...).\\n' +\n    ' * 6. TIME. game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or game.start({ scenes, scene }).\\n' +\n    ' *    update runs in exact fixed steps: each 1/120 s of real time is one update with\\n' +\n    ' *    dt = 1/120 * timeScale, so motion is identical at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz (game.alpha =\\n' +\n    ' *    the part of a step left over). game.freeze(s) = hit-stop, game.shake(n) = screen shake,\\n' +\n    ' *    game.timeScale = slow motion, game.paused = pause, game.after(s, fn) = timer.\\n' +\n    ' * 7. LEVELS. Top-down / isometric / brawler floors: E.TileMap (walls on the ground\\n' +\n    ' *    plane). Side-scrollers: E.PlatformMap + E.Platformer (tiles on the x/z plane,\\n' +\n    ' *    solid, one-way, ladders, hazards, moving platforms; crouching, ledges, clinging, water, ropes, bars, swinging and\\n' +\n    \" *    momentum on request: 16c). Both read ASCII rows. Third person (Assassin's Creed):\\n\" +\n    ' *    E.Mover on a TileMap of roofs climbs, vaults and leaps; E.ACTIONS, and E.sees / E.Suspicion for guards (17b-17d).\\n' +\n    \" * 8. SOUND, TEXT, MENUS. game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song);\\n\" +\n    ' *    E.font.text(...) (5x7 proportional font), E.font.title(...), E.ui.box/bar/hearts,\\n' +\n    ' *    new E.Dialog(game) (markup: {c=#hex}, {w=.5}, {s=12}, {shake}, {wave}, {$name}), new E.Menu(game, items). An HTML\\n' +\n    ' *    interface (title, pause, settings, help, results, map screens and a HUD with a minimap) from data: new E.Shell(game,\\n' +\n    ' *    {...}) (21d). Save slots: game.saves (10k).\\n' +\n    ' * 8a. STANDARD FEATURES come with every game (10b): a pause menu with settings and controls the player rebinds, saved\\n' +\n    \" *    per game (game.settings), full screen (game.fullscreen; a phone's first tap), pausing when the page is hidden, and\\n\" +\n    ' *    input.label(action) for prompts, and a boot (10c): heavy setup in game.start({ boot: { steps } }) runs behind a\\n' +\n    \" *    boot screen, and an error before play shows a report to screenshot. Don't build your own: adjust them with\\n\" +\n    ' *    new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit, saves } }), or leave them out (menu: false, fullscreen: false,\\n' +\n    ' *    corner: false, autoPause: false).\\n' +\n    ' * 8b. LOOK. Aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES: HD rigs (size, outfit, hair, armor),\\n' +\n    \" *    new E.Backdrop('castle-night') behind levels, textured tiles (stone, brick, wood,\\n\" +\n    \" *    metal), r.prop('torch', ...) set dressing, particles.explosion / smoke / fire,\\n\" +\n    ' *    game.flash, and E.tones(hex) / E.ramp(hex) for hue-shifted shading.\\n' +\n    ' * 9. GPU LIGHTING is optional: game.enableGPU({ map }). Mark glowing things with\\n' +\n    ' *    px.glow(g, 1) inside queue callbacks, pass { color, shadow } to lights.add, and\\n' +\n    ' *    register shadow casters with game.lights.caster(). Falls back to Canvas lighting.\\n' +\n    ' * 10. SESSIONS. E.session.record(true) records every later page load (or ?record=1 for one): the frame times, the\\n' +\n    ' *    inputs, the random seed, the screen and the save. E.session.share() saves it as a file; node tools/replay.mjs\\n' +\n    ' *    plays it back headless, exactly, and stops where it differs (game.stateHash = () => [...] sharpens that).\\n' +\n    ' * 11. TESTS. new E.Game({ headless: true }) (the default in Node) runs with no screen: game.advance(1) runs 120 fixed\\n' +\n    \" *    steps at once, game.input.tap('jump') and hold('right') script input, game.debug = { snapshot() { ... } } is\\n\" +\n    \" *    your game's handle for tests and tools; game.stop(), start() and dispose() stop, resume and end it.\\n\" +\n    ' * Errors in your game are shown on screen and in the console (game.errors).\\n' +\n    ' * THE HANDOFF PACKAGE holds this file and what it needs (dist/handoff/ in the repo, my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/):\\n' +\n    ' *    API.md, the card (read it whole; the single-file editions embed it), API-INDEX.md (every public name, one line\\n' +\n    ' *    each: search it), AI_GUIDE.md (the recipes), tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs and replay.mjs, and game/, a\\n' +\n    ' *    template game with a headless test.\\n' +\n    ' * MORE, outside this file (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; search it and take only what a game needs, its files\\n' +\n    ' *    are large): 325 curated motion clips and 2,548 motion-capture takes as readable key poses that play on any\\n' +\n    \" *    Humanoid (src/mocap/: the catalogs list every clip's tags and description, tools/anim-set.mjs cuts the ones you\\n\" +\n    ' *    want into a small set, src/mocap/mocap.js plays them), and Emberdeep (src/emberdeep/), a whole game of worked\\n' +\n    ' *    examples: monsters, bosses, heroes with bodies that are not people, skills. AI_GUIDE.md, \"Where to find more\",\\n' +\n    ' *    says how to search them and take what you need. Still objects and materials as data (r.shape, E.MATERIALS):\\n' +\n    ' *    docs/ASSETS.md, its recipe checked with tools/asset-sheet.mjs.\\n' +\n    ' */\\n' +\n    '(function (root) {\\n' +\n    \"'use strict';\\n\" +\n    '\\n' +\n    '/** TAU: 2π, a full turn in radians; DEG: π/180, one degree in radians (angle * DEG) */\\n' +\n    'const TAU = Ma