opped, and shoot up at two deckhands on the turn above; the ride into 2-B climbs the rest) |\n| 64 | The attract mode's DEMO PLAY bar hid the HUD's score and TIME labels; the touch-only notice was drawn over the TIME plate |\n\"\"\"\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new)\nold=s[s.index(\"## Totals\"):]\nnew=\"\"\"## Totals\n- **65 issues caught by testing**: 57 in the game (22 from the bot runs, 10 from the content validator, 2 from the\n  lighthouse and tower-roof checks, 1 from the UI suite, 4 from the browser end-to-end suite, 18 from screenshots) and 8\n  in the bot used for testing.\n- **13 critical** (1 to 13).\n- Found by reviewing the build against the plan rather than by a test (tests added since): only 4 of the 6 intel\n  bottles were placed; the first ARCADE start did not offer Training.\n- Caught nothing: the 3D math and rasterizer unit tests (`math3d`, `raster`).\n\"\"\"\ns=s.replace(old,new)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='src/99-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\nold=\"Used: node tests/run.mjs, 26 suites: 23 headless (core, gameplay, content, UI, dev, attract, co-op and a FakeHub net suite with latency, jitter and loss) plus --playthrough (the expert bot clears every stage, the fair bot clears every stage on Normal without a continue, the showoff bot plays the whole ARCADE with its cutscenes, Training completes), --browser (the bundle at four window sizes) and --hub (the four AI Hub harness scenarios in headless Chromium); tests/ISSUES.md logs what they caught\"\nnew=\"Used: node tests/run.mjs, 28 suites: 24 headless (core, gameplay, content, the lighthouse climb and the tower roof, UI, dev, attract, co-op and a FakeHub net suite with latency, jitter and loss) plus --playthrough (the expert bot clears every stage, the fair bot clears every stage on Normal without a continue, the showoff bot plays the whole ARCADE with its cutscenes, Training completes), --browser (the bundle at four window sizes, then an end-to-end run with real key presses, mouse, touch and a stubbed DualSense: the attract mode, every About page, prompts following the device, dev mode from the pause menu, detail per stage, a touch-only phone) and --hub (the four AI Hub harness scenarios in headless Chromium); tests/ISSUES.md logs what they caught\"\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new)\nold=\"'56, itemised in tests/ISSUES.md: 48 in the game (22 caught by the bot playthroughs and boss runs, 10 cover points caught by the content validator, 1 caught by the UI suite, 15 caught by screenshots) and 8 in the bot used for testing.'\"\nnew=\"'65, itemised in tests/ISSUES.md: 57 in the game (22 caught by the bot playthroughs and boss runs, 10 cover points caught by the content validator, 2 caught by the lighthouse and tower-roof checks, 1 caught by the UI suite, 4 caught by the browser end-to-end suite, 18 caught by screenshots) and 8 in the bot used for testing.'\"\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"engine: \\[\" -A3 src/99-about.js | cut -c1-600","description":"Update issue log and About testing text"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0134zpW9tJQbqXSKN3fkNszU","type":"tool_result","content":"27:  engine: [\n28-    'The deterministic fixed-step loop with headless game.advance, and the Humanoid rigs: they update the same in Node and in the browser, so hit volumes from real joints, the bots and the AI Hub replicas all agree, and the same rigs draw from any bearing as billboards.',\n29-    'Yes. The handoff has no 3D engine, so a first-person light-gun game needed its own renderer. E.View cannot look up (pitch is clamped), so billboards fake the angle. A rig update with vx but no vy put NaN into the rig\\'s walk phase for good, which made a boss unhittable until a test found it. The Humanoid constructor draws from Math.random, so the game reseeds each rig\\'s clock for determinism.',\n30-    'Rail3D: a z-buffered, perspective-correct software rasterizer that samples E.materialTile textures in the engine\\'s five light bands with Bayer dithering and fog, draws E.shapeMesh objects, and composites engine rigs as depth-tested billboards drawn by the engine from the bearing they are seen at.',","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcbe5b02-218b-4276-a1cd-eeebf017e39a","uuid":"50211e40-9344-4ae3-aefe-9cb4a681a9aa","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:26:32.735Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"27:  engine: [\n28-    'The deterministic fixed-step loop with headless game.advance, and the Humanoid rigs: they update the same in Node and in the browser, so hit volumes from real joints, the bots and the AI Hub replicas all agree, and the same rigs draw from any bearing as billboards.',\n29-    'Yes. The handoff has no 3D engine, so a first-person light-gun game needed its own renderer. E.View cannot look up (pitch is clamped), so billboards fake the angle. A rig update with vx but no vy put NaN into the rig\\'s walk phase for good, which made a boss unhittable until a test found it. The Humanoid constructor draws from Math.random, so the game reseeds each rig\\'s clock for determinism.',\n30-    'Rail3D: a z-buffered, perspective-correct software rasterizer that samples E.materialTile textures in the engine\\'s five light bands with Bayer dithering and fog, draws E.shapeMesh objects, and composites engine rigs as depth-tested billboards drawn by the engine from the bearing they are seen at.',","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0134zpW9tJQbqXSKN3fkNszU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
